// Class 11 Physics Unit I: Physical World and Measurement (05 Marks)
// Units and Measurements (JKBOSE / NCERT Official Syllabus Alignment)
// Gold Standard Reference Textbook & 3-Tab Architecture (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora Standard)

export const c11Phy1HtmlOverview = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY & BASIC DEFINITIONS -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary & Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts & Foundational Terms &bull; Unit I: Physical World and Measurement</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Physical Quantity:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Any measurable parameter of nature or matter that can be quantified numerically and described by physical laws (e.g. Mass, Length, Time, Force).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Standard Unit of Measurement:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">An internationally chosen, invariant, reproducible reference standard of known magnitude used to express physical quantities quantitatively.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">3. Fundamental (Base) Units:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Seven mutually independent primary units (Length, Mass, Time, Current, Temp, Amount of Substance, Luminous Intensity) forming the bedrock of SI.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">4. Derived Units:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Secondary units derived algebraically from combinations of base units (e.g., Velocity m s<sup>-1</sup>, Force kg m s<sup>-2</sup> = N, Pressure Pa).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">5. Significant Figures:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The count of meaningful digits in an experimentally measured quantity that are reliably certain, plus the single terminal digit that contains uncertainty.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">6. Dimensions of a Quantity:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The powers (exponents) to which the fundamental base units [M], [L], [T], [I], [&theta;], [N], [J] must be raised to represent that physical quantity.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">7. Principle of Homogeneity:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Fundamental law asserting that in any physically valid equation, every additive, subtractive, or equated term must possess identical dimensions.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">8. Supplementary Units:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Radian (rad) for plane angle (ds/r) and Steradian (sr) for solid angle (dA/r<sup>2</sup>), which are dimensionless physical quantities [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1: NEED FOR MEASUREMENT -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    1. Need for Measurement in Physics\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>Physics is the quantitative empirical science dedicated to deciphering the fundamental principles governing matter, energy, space, and time. Human physiological senses—such as sight, touch, and hearing—are subjective, qualitative, and notoriously vulnerable to optical illusions and physiological biases. For example, judging the temperature of lukewarm water by touching it with cold versus warm hands yields contradictory sensory reports. As Lord Kelvin famously remarked: <i>\"When you can measure what you are speaking about, and express it in numbers, you know something about it; but when you cannot measure it, when you cannot express it in numbers, your knowledge is of a meager and unsatisfactory kind.\"</i></p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Physical Quantity (Q)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">A property of an object, material, or natural phenomenon that can be measured using an appropriate scientific instrument and quantified in terms of a numerical value and a unit.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Dependent on chosen standard (e.g., kg, m, s)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Dimensions:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b</sup> T<sup>c</sup> ...]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) The Fundamental Equation of Measurement</h3>\n  <p>Every scientific measurement expresses the magnitude of a physical quantity Q as the mathematical product of a pure numerical value n (magnitude) and a chosen standard reference unit u:</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">\n    <b>Q = n &times; u &nbsp; &rArr; &nbsp; n<sub>1</sub> u<sub>1</sub> = n<sub>2</sub> u<sub>2</sub> = \text{Constant} &nbsp; &rArr; &nbsp; n &prop; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u</span></span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>This inverse relationship reveals that if a smaller unit of measurement is adopted, the corresponding numerical magnitude increases proportionally. For example, a length of 5 meters (n_1 = 5, u_1 = \text{m}) translates to 500 centimeters (n_2 = 500, u_2 = \text{cm}). The intrinsic physical magnitude of the measured object remains invariant under changes in coordinate system or unit scale.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Currency Exchange & Invariance of Magnitude</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Measuring a length in meters versus centimeters is directly analogous to paying for an item in 100-rupee notes versus 1-rupee coins. The total monetary value remains invariant (Q = Constant); only the count of units (n) scales inversely with the size of the currency unit (u).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: UNITS OF MEASUREMENT -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    2. Units of Measurement\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>To communicate measurements globally and verify natural laws reproducibly across international laboratories, the scientific community establishes standard reference definitions termed <b>units</b>.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Essential Characteristics of an Ideal Standard Unit</h3>\n  <p>For a reference quantity to serve as an acceptable scientific standard, it must fulfill rigorous metrological criteria:</p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">1. Invariability & Constancy:</b> The standard unit must never alter its magnitude with fluctuations in ambient temperature, atmospheric pressure, geographical location, gravitational potential, or passage of historical time.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">2. Universal Reproducibility:</b> Scientists worldwide must be able to reproduce or replicate the unit in standard calibration laboratories without requiring physical access to a fragile single prototype.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">3. Indestructibility:</b> It must not degrade, oxidize, wear out, or corrode over centuries.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">4. Convenient Size:</b> Its physical dimension should be of appropriate scale so that everyday practical laboratory measurements do not produce unwieldy, astronomically large or infinitesimally small numerical values.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">5. Unambiguous Precision:</b> The physical phenomenon or atomic property defining the unit must be mathematically and experimentally distinct, leaving zero margin for subjective interpretation.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: SYSTEMS OF UNITS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    3. Systems of Units\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>Prior to international standardization, different nations and scientific communities relied upon divergent regional systems of units:</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Historical Metric & Imperial Systems</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">1. CGS System (Centimeter-Gram-Second):</b> Originated in France (Gaussian system). Measures length in centimeters (cm), mass in grams (g), and time in seconds (s). Standard unit of force is Dyne (10<sup>-5</sup> N), and work is Erg (10<sup>-7</sup> J).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">2. FPS System (Foot-Pound-Second):</b> British Imperial system. Measures length in feet (ft), mass in pounds (lb), and time in seconds (s). Lacks decimal coherence and requires complex conversion factors (e.g., 1 foot = 12 inches, 1 yard = 3 feet).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">3. MKS System (Meter-Kilogram-Second):</b> Formulated by Giovanni Giorgi in 1901. Measures length in meters (m), mass in kilograms (kg), and time in seconds (s). This system provided the direct mechanical foundation for the modern SI system.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: SI UNITS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    4. The International System of Units (Syst&egrave;me International d'Unit&eacute;s - SI)\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>In 1971, the 14th General Conference on Weights and Measures (CGPM - <i>Conf&eacute;rence G&eacute;n&eacute;rale des Poids et Mesures</i>) formalized the <b>SI System</b> to establish an international scientific lingua franca. In 2019, the CGPM enacted a historic revision of the SI system, redefining all base units by permanently locking in the exact numerical values of fundamental physical constants (c, h, e, k_B, N_A, Delta\nu_{Cs}, K_{cd}), severing all reliance on physical manufactured artifacts like the platinum-iridium kilogram prototype in S&egrave;vres, France.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) The 7 Fundamental SI Base Quantities & Exact Modern Definitions</h3>\n  <div style=\"margin-left: 10px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <p><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">1. Length &bull; Meter (m):</b> The path distance traveled by electromagnetic radiation (light) in vacuum during a time interval of exactly <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">299,792,458</span></span> of a second, fixing speed of light c = 299,792,458 \text{ m s}<sup>-1</sup>.</p>\n    <p><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">2. Mass &bull; Kilogram (kg):</b> Defined by setting the Planck constant h to exactly 6.62607015 \times 10<sup>-34</sup> \text{ J s} = \text{kg m}<sup>2</sup> \text{s}<sup>-1</sup>, measured via the Kibble balance.</p>\n    <p><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">3. Time &bull; Second (s):</b> Defined by taking the fixed unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition frequency of the Caesium-133 atom (Delta\nu_{Cs}) to be exactly 9,192,631,770 \text{ Hz}.</p>\n    <p><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">4. Electric Current &bull; Ampere (A):</b> Defined by fixing the elementary charge e = 1.602176634 \times 10<sup>-19</sup> \text{ C}, where 1 \text{ A} = 1 \text{ C s}<sup>-1</sup>.</p>\n    <p><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">5. Thermodynamic Temperature &bull; Kelvin (K):</b> Defined by fixing the Boltzmann constant k_B = 1.380649 \times 10<sup>-23</sup> \text{ J K}<sup>-1</sup> = \text{kg m}<sup>2</sup> \text{s}<sup>-2</sup> \text{K}<sup>-1</sup>.</p>\n    <p><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">6. Amount of Substance &bull; Mole (mol):</b> One mole contains exactly 6.02214076 \times 10<sup>23</sup> elementary entities (Avogadro constant N_A).</p>\n    <p><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">7. Luminous Intensity &bull; Candela (cd):</b> The luminous intensity, in a given direction, of a monochromatic radiation source of frequency 540 \times 10<sup>12</sup> \text{ Hz} that has a radiant intensity in that direction of <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">683</span></span> \text{ W sr}<sup>-1</sup>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Supplementary Dimensionless Quantities: Radian & Steradian</h3>\n  <p>In addition to the 7 base units, SI incorporates two geometric supplementary quantities:</p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Plane Angle &bull; Radian (rad):</b> The angle subtended at the center of a circular arc of length equal to its radius:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0;\"><b>d&theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> \text{ rad} &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; 2pi \text{ rad} = 360<sup>c</sup>irc</b></div>\n      Because it is the ratio of two lengths ([L] / [L]), radian has unit 'rad' but is <b>dimensionless</b>: [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Solid Angle &bull; Steradian (sr):</b> The 3D conical solid angle subtended at the center of a sphere of radius r by a surface area dA = r<sup>2</sup>:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0;\"><b>d&Omega; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dA</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> \text{ sr} &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; Omega_{\text{sphere}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4pi r<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> = 4pi \text{ sr}</b></div>\n      Because it is the ratio of area to radius squared ([L<sup>2</sup>] / [L<sup>2</sup>]), steradian has unit 'sr' but is <b>dimensionless</b>: [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.08); border: 1.2px solid #EF4444; border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM FOCUS & COMMON MISCONCEPTION: Dimensionless Quantities Can Possess Units!</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FEE2E2; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">A frequent board exam question asks: <i>'Can a physical quantity possess a unit but have no dimensions?'</i> The answer is an emphatic <b>YES</b>! Plane angle (radian) and Solid angle (steradian) have internationally recognized SI units, but their dimensional formulas are strictly [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(iii) Core Advantages of the SI System</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">1. Coherent System:</b> All derived units are obtained solely by simple algebraic multiplication or division of base units without introducing awkward arbitrary numerical constants (e.g. 1 \text{ N} = 1 \text{ kg} \times 1 \text{ m s}<sup>-2</sup>).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">2. Rational System:</b> SI assigns only one unique unit to any given physical quantity across all scientific disciplines. In SI, mechanical work, kinetic energy, gravitational potential energy, electric energy, and heat energy are all measured uniformly in <b>Joules (J)</b>, whereas older systems used calories, ergs, and foot-pounds.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">3. Absolute Metric System:</b> Decimal-based multiplication/division using powers of 10 (10<sup>-3</sup>, 10<sup>-6</sup>, 10<sup>3</sup>, 10<sup>6</sup>) eliminates complex fractions. It is independent of local acceleration due to gravity g.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(iv) Practical Astronomical & Microscopic Units</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 15px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Astronomical Unit (AU):</b> Mean orbital distance between Earth and Sun: 1 \text{ AU} = 1.496 \times 10<sup>11</sup> \text{ m}.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Light Year (ly):</b> Distance light travels in vacuum in 1 Julian year (365.25 days): 1 \text{ ly} = c \times t = (3 \times 10<sup>8</sup>) \times (365.25 \times 86400) = 9.46 \times 10<sup>15</sup> \text{ m}.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Parsec (pc):</b> Distance at which an astronomical arc of 1 AU subtends a parallax angle of 1 arcsecond (1''): 1 \text{ pc} = {frac('1 \text{ AU}', '1 \text{ arcsec}')} = 3.08 \times 10<sup>16</sup> \text{ m} approx 3.26 \text{ ly}.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Fermi (Femtometer, fm):</b> Nuclear scale unit: 1 \text{ fm} = 10<sup>-15</sup> \text{ m}.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Angstrom (&Aring;):</b> Atomic scale unit: 1 \text{ AA} = 10<sup>-10</sup> \text{ m} = 0.1 \text{ nm}.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Chandrasekhar Limit (CSL):</b> Practical astronomical unit of stellar mass: 1 \text{ CSL} = 1.4 \times M_{\text{Sun}} approx 2.8 \times 10<sup>30</sup> \text{ kg}.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Shake:</b> High-speed nuclear decay time unit: 1 \text{ shake} = 10<sup>-8</sup> \text{ s}.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5: FUNDAMENTAL AND DERIVED UNITS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    5. Fundamental and Derived Quantities\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>In physics, every observed phenomenon is parameterized through either independent foundational variables or composite derived quantities:</p>\n\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #FF512F;\">Parameter</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #FF512F;\">Fundamental Quantities</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #FF512F;\">Derived Quantities</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Operational Nature</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Completely independent of all other physical quantities. Cannot be simplified further.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Expressed as mathematical products, quotients, or powers of base quantities.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Total Number in SI</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Strictly limited to 7 base quantities + 2 supplementary.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Infinite (covers all mechanics, optics, thermodynamics, electromagnetism, and modern physics).</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Examples</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Length (m), Mass (kg), Time (s), Current (A), Temperature (K), Substance (mol), Intensity (cd).</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Force (N = kg m s<sup>-2</sup>), Energy (J = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>), Electric Potential (V = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>).</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6: SIGNIFICANT FIGURES -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    6. Significant Figures & Rules of Scientific Rounding\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>Experimental science deals with measurements recorded via instruments of finite least count. <b>Significant figures</b> in an experimental measurement communicate both the magnitude and the precision of the measuring instrument. Significant figures include all digits that are known with experimental certainty plus one final estimated digit that contains inherent uncertainty.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) The 6 Universal Rules for Determining Significant Figures</h3>\n  <ol style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Rule 1 &bull; All Non-Zero Digits are Significant:</b> In the measured length 178.46 \text{ cm}, all 5 digits are non-zero &rArr; <b>5 significant figures</b>.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Rule 2 &bull; All Trapped Zeros Between Non-Zero Digits are Significant:</b> Zeros situated between non-zero digits are always significant regardless of decimal position (e.g. 4007 \text{ kg} has <b>4 sig figs</b>; 3.0004 \text{ s} has <b>5 sig figs</b>).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Rule 3 &bull; Leading (Initial) Zeros are NEVER Significant:</b> Zeros appearing before the first non-zero digit merely fix the position of the decimal point (e.g. 0.0035 \text{ m} has only <b>2 sig figs</b>: 3 and 5; 0.000804 \text{ g} has <b>3 sig figs</b>: 8, 0, 4).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Rule 4 &bull; Trailing Zeros in Numbers With a Decimal are ALWAYS Significant:</b> Zeros at the end of a decimal number reflect measuring instrument precision (e.g. 4.500 \text{ cm} has <b>4 sig figs</b>; 0.07000 \text{ V} has <b>4 sig figs</b>).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Rule 5 &bull; Trailing Zeros in Numbers WITHOUT a Decimal are Ambiguous:</b> In integers like 4500, trailing zeros are generally not considered significant unless clarified by a decimal (4500. has 4) or expressed in scientific notation (4.5 \times 10<sup>3</sup> has 2; 4.500 \times 10<sup>3</sup> has 4).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Rule 6 &bull; Exact Numbers & Mathematical Constants Have Infinite (&infin;) Significant Figures:</b> Exact integers (e.g., 2 in 2pi r, 10 test tubes, speed of light defined as exact) possess infinite precision: 2.000000....</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Standard Rules for Rounding Off Measurements</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li>If the digit to be dropped is less than 5, the preceding digit is left unchanged (7.84 rounds to 7.8).</li>\n    <li>If the digit to be dropped is greater than 5, the preceding digit is increased by 1 (7.86 rounds to 7.9).</li>\n    <li>If the digit to be dropped is 5 followed by non-zero digits, the preceding digit is increased by 1 (14.251 rounds to 14.3).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">The Even-Odd Rule (Preceding a Terminal 5):</b> If the digit to be dropped is simply 5 or 5 followed by zeros:\n      <br>&bull; If the preceding digit is <b>EVEN</b>, it is left unchanged (6.450 \to 6.4).\n      <br>&bull; If the preceding digit is <b>ODD</b>, it is rounded up by 1 to make it even (6.350 \to 6.4).\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(iii) Rules for Arithmetic Operations with Significant Figures</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Addition and Subtraction:</b> The final result cannot be more precise than the least precise measurement. The final answer must be rounded off to the <b>least number of decimal places</b> present in any term.\n      <br><i>Example:</i> 4.327 \text{ g} + 2.1 \text{ g} + 0.05 \text{ g} = 6.477 \text{ g} \to mathbf{6.5 \text{ g}} (governed by 2.1 with 1 decimal place).\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Multiplication and Division:</b> The final calculated product or quotient must be rounded off to contain only as many significant figures as are present in the measurement with the <b>least number of significant figures</b>.\n      <br><i>Example:</i> Mass = 4.237 \text{ g} (4 sig figs), Volume = 2.5 \text{ cm}<sup>3</sup> (2 sig figs).\n      <br>Density = {frac('4.237', '2.5')} = 1.6948 \text{ g cm}<sup>-3</sup> \to mathbf{1.7 \text{ g cm}<sup>-3</sup>} (rounded to 2 sig figs).\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7: DIMENSIONS OF PHYSICAL QUANTITIES -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    7. Dimensions of Physical Quantities & Dimensional Formulas\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>The <b>dimensions</b> of any physical quantity are the powers to which base quantities must be raised to represent that physical quantity. The symbolic notation enclosed within brackets [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b</sup> T<sup>c</sup> I<sup>d</sup> Theta<sup>e</sup> N<sup>f</sup> J<sup>g</sup>] is designated as the <b>dimensional formula</b>.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Dimensional Formula</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">An algebraic expression showing which fundamental base quantities and to what powers (exponents) enter into the derived unit of a given physical quantity.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">None (Pure algebraic exponent representation)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Dimensions:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b</sup> T<sup>c</sup> I<sup>d</sup> &Theta;<sup>e</sup> N<sup>f</sup> J<sup>g</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Master Dimensional Table of Core Physical Quantities</h3>\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); font-size: 14px;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 9px; text-align: left; color: #FF512F;\">Physical Quantity</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 9px; text-align: left; color: #FF512F;\">Governing Relationship</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 9px; text-align: left; color: #FF512F;\">Dimensional Formula</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 9px; text-align: left; color: #FF512F;\">SI Unit</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Velocity / Speed (v)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Distance / Time</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L T<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">m s<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Acceleration (a)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Velocity / Time</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L T<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">m s<sup>-2</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Force / Weight (F)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Mass &times; Acceleration</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L T<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Newton (N)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Work / Energy / Heat / Torque</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Force &times; Distance</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Joule (J) / N m</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Power (P)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Work / Time</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Watt (W)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Pressure / Stress / Elastic Modulus (Y, B, eta)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Force / Area</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Pascal (Pa) / N m<sup>-2</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Linear Momentum / Impulse (p, J)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Mass &times; Velocity / Force &times; Time</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L T<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">kg m s<sup>-1</sup> / N s</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Universal Gravitational Constant (G)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">F r<sup>2</sup> / (m_1 m_2)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03;\">[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Planck's Constant (h) & Angular Momentum (L)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">E / \nu / m v r</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">J s / kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Surface Tension (T) & Spring Constant (k)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Force / Length</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">N m<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Coefficient of Viscosity (eta)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">F / [A (dv/dx)] (Stokes' Law)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03;\">[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Pa s / Poiseuille (Pl)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Thermal Conductivity (K)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Q d / [A (T_1 - T_2) t]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L T<sup>-3</sup> &Theta;<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">W m<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Specific Heat Capacity (s, c)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Q / (m Delta T)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> &Theta;<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">J kg<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Latent Heat (L)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Q / m</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">J kg<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Universal Gas Constant (R) & Boltzmann Constant (k_B)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">P V / (n T) / P V / (N T)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> &Theta;<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup> / J K<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Electric Charge (q)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Current &times; Time (I \times t)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T A]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Coulomb (C)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Electric Potential / EMF (V)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Work / Charge (W / q)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #00E5FF;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Volt (V)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Permittivity of Free Space (\u000barepsilon_0)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">q_1 q_2 / (4pi F r<sup>2</sup>)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03;\">[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">C<sup>2</sup> N<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-2</sup> / F m<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Permeability of Free Space (mu_0)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">2pi r F / (I_1 I_2 L)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03;\">[M L T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">T m A<sup>-1</sup> / H m<sup>-1</sup></td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Classification: Dimensional/Dimensionless Quantities</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">1. Dimensional Variables:</b> Physical quantities that vary in magnitude and possess specific dimensions (e.g. Velocity [L T<sup>-1</sup>], Acceleration [L T<sup>-2</sup>], Force [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">2. Dimensionless Variables:</b> Physical parameters that take variable values but possess zero dimensions [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>] (e.g. Specific gravity, Strain Delta L / L, Refractive index mu, Angle \theta).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">3. Dimensional Constants:</b> Universal or empirical constants having fixed numerical values that carry dimensions (e.g. Universal Gravitational Constant G, Planck constant h, Speed of light in vacuum c, Boltzmann constant k_B).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">4. Dimensionless Constants:</b> Pure numerical constants possessing fixed values and zero dimensions (e.g. pi = 3.14159..., Euler's number e = 2.718..., pure numbers 1, 2, {frac('1', '2')}).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8: DIMENSIONAL ANALYSIS AND APPLICATIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    8. Dimensional Analysis and Its Three Core Applications\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>Dimensional analysis represents one of the most powerful algebraic techniques in theoretical physics, founded upon the foundational <b>Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity</b>.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">A physical equation is dimensionally correct if and only if the dimensions of all individual terms on both sides of the equation (separated by +, -, or =) are strictly identical. One can only add, subtract, or equate terms that possess identical dimensions.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">None</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">Dimensions:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">For A + B = C - D &rArr; [A] = [B] = [C] = [D]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 22px;\">Application 1: Checking the Correctness / Consistency of Equations</h3>\n  <p>To verify if an equation is dimensionally consistent, we evaluate the dimensional formula of each additive term on the Left Hand Side (LHS) and Right Hand Side (RHS). If any term disagrees, the equation is unphysical and incorrect.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Checking Kinematic Equation s = u t + ½ a t²\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: LHS Dimensional Evaluation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Displacement term on LHS: [\text{LHS}] = [s] = [L] = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: RHS First Term (u t) Evaluation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Initial velocity times time: [u cdot t] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>] \times [T] = [L T<sup>(-1+1)</sup>] = [L] = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: RHS Second Term (½ a t²) Evaluation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The numerical factor ½ is a pure dimensionless constant [½] = [1].\n          <br>[a cdot t<sup>2</sup>] = [L T<sup>-2</sup>] \times [T<sup>2</sup>] = [L T<sup>(-2+2)</sup>] = [L] = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 4: Conclusion by Principle of Homogeneity</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Since [\text{LHS}] = [u cdot t] = [½ a t<sup>2</sup>] = [L], every term has identical dimensions of length. Therefore, the equation is <b>dimensionally consistent and valid</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 22px;\">Application 2: Deducing Relations Among Physical Quantities</h3>\n  <p>If we know the physical parameters upon which a certain quantity depends, we express the relationship as a power law with unknown exponents (a, b, c) and apply dimensional homogeneity to solve for the exponents.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Deriving Formula for Time Period of Simple Pendulum (T = 2π √(l/g))\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: Hypothesis Formulation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Assume time period T of a simple pendulum depends upon:\n          <br>(1) Mass of bob m raised to power a: m<sup>a</sup>\n          <br>(2) Effective length of pendulum l raised to power b: l<sup>b</sup>\n          <br>(3) Acceleration due to gravity g raised to power c: g<sup>c</sup>\n          <br>Hence: <b>T = k &middot; m<sup>a</sup> &middot; l<sup>b</sup> &middot; g<sup>c</sup></b>, where k is a dimensionless constant.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: Substitute Dimensional Formulas</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">LHS: [T] = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>1</sup>]\n          <br>RHS: [k] [m]<sup>a</sup> [l]<sup>b</sup> [g]<sup>c</sup> = [1] [M]<sup>a</sup> [L]<sup>b</sup> [L T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>(b+c)</sup> T<sup>-2c</sup>].\n          <br>Equating LHS and RHS: <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>1</sup>] = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b+c</sup> T<sup>-2c</sup>]</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: Equate Exponents of Fundamental Quantities</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">&bull; For mass [M]: a = 0 (Time period is independent of bob mass!)\n          <br>&bull; For time [T]: -2c = 1 implies c = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>\n          <br>&bull; For length [L]: b + c = 0 implies b = -c = +<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 4: Assemble Final Mathematical Relationship</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Substitute exponents back into formula:\n          <br>T = k cdot m<sup>0</sup> cdot l<sup>1/2</sup> cdot g<sup>-1/2</sup> = k sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>}.\n          <br>Experimental measurement establishes k = 2pi, yielding the celebrated formula:\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">T = 2pi sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>}</div></div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Deriving Centripetal Force (F = m v² / r)\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: Formulate Power Law Dependence</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Centripetal force F of a body moving in a circular path depends on:\n          <br>Mass m<sup>a</sup>, orbital velocity v<sup>b</sup>, and orbit radius r<sup>c</sup>.\n          <br>Equation: <b>F = k &middot; m<sup>a</sup> &middot; v<sup>b</sup> &middot; r<sup>c</sup></b> (where k is a dimensionless constant).</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: Substitute Dimensions & Group Terms</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">LHS: [F] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]\n          <br>RHS: [M]<sup>a</sup> [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>b</sup> [L]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>(b+c)</sup> T<sup>-b</sup>].\n          <br>Equating dimensions: <b>[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b+c</sup> T<sup>-b</sup>]</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: Solve System of Linear Equations</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">&bull; Comparing power of [M]: a = 1\n          <br>&bull; Comparing power of [T]: -b = -2 implies b = 2\n          <br>&bull; Comparing power of [L]: b + c = 1 implies 2 + c = 1 implies c = -1.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #FF512F;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 4: Final Formula Assembly</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Substitute a=1, b=2, c=-1:\n          <br>F = k cdot m<sup>1</sup> cdot v<sup>2</sup> cdot r<sup>-1</sup> = k <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span>.\n          <br>In SI units, experimentation confirms k = 1, yielding:\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 22px;\">Application 3: Conversion of a Physical Unit From One System to Another</h3>\n  <p>Based on the universal invariance of physical magnitude (Q = n_1 u_1 = n_2 u_2), if a quantity has dimensions [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b</sup> T<sup>c</sup>], its numerical value n_2 in a second system with base units (M_2, L_2, T_2) relates to n_1 in the first system (M_1, L_1, T_1) by:</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">\n    <b>n<sub>2</sub> = n<sub>1</sub> &times; left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right]<sup>a</sup> &times; left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right]<sup>b</sup> &times; left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right]<sup>c</sup></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 215, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 15.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📝 WORKED BOARD NUMERICAL EXAMPLE 1</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><b>Problem:</b> Convert 1 Joule of energy from the SI system into Ergs in the CGS system using dimensional analysis.</div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); border-left: 3px solid #FFD700; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FFD700; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">Solution & Step-by-Step Calculation:</b>\n      Energy dimensional formula is [E] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] implies a = 1, b = 2, c = -2.\n    <br>&bull; <b>System 1 (SI):</b> n_1 = 1, M_1 = 1 \text{ kg} = 1000 \text{ g}, L_1 = 1 \text{ m} = 100 \text{ cm}, T_1 = 1 \text{ s}.\n    <br>&bull; <b>System 2 (CGS):</b> n_2 = ?, M_2 = 1 \text{ g}, L_2 = 1 \text{ cm}, T_2 = 1 \text{ s}.\n    <br>Apply unit conversion master relation:\n    <br>n_2 = 1 \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1000 \text{ g}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ g}</span></span>\right]<sup>1</sup> \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">100 \text{ cm}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ cm}</span></span>\right]<sup>2</sup> \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ s}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ s}</span></span>\right]<sup>-2</sup>\n    <br>n_2 = 1 \times (10<sup>3</sup>)<sup>1</sup> \times (10<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> \times (1)<sup>-2</sup> = 10<sup>3</sup> \times 10<sup>4</sup> \times 1 = mathbf{10<sup>7</sup>}.\n    <br>Therefore: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\">1 Joule = 10<sup>7</sup> Erg</span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 215, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 15.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📝 WORKED BOARD NUMERICAL EXAMPLE 2</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><b>Problem:</b> Convert 1 Newton of force from SI into Dyne in CGS units.</div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); border-left: 3px solid #FFD700; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FFD700; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">Solution & Step-by-Step Calculation:</b>\n      Force dimensional formula is [F] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>] implies a = 1, b = 1, c = -2.\n    <br>&bull; <b>System 1 (SI):</b> n_1 = 1, M_1 = 1 \text{ kg} = 1000 \text{ g}, L_1 = 1 \text{ m} = 100 \text{ cm}, T_1 = 1 \text{ s}.\n    <br>&bull; <b>System 2 (CGS):</b> n_2 = ?, M_2 = 1 \text{ g}, L_2 = 1 \text{ cm}, T_2 = 1 \text{ s}.\n    <br>n_2 = 1 \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1000 \text{ g}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ g}</span></span>\right]<sup>1</sup> \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">100 \text{ cm}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ cm}</span></span>\right]<sup>1</sup> \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ s}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 \text{ s}</span></span>\right]<sup>-2</sup>\n    <br>n_2 = 1 \times 10<sup>3</sup> \times 10<sup>2</sup> \times 1 = mathbf{10<sup>5</sup>}.\n    <br>Therefore: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\">1 Newton = 10<sup>5</sup> Dyne</span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 22px;\">(iv) Critical Limitations of Dimensional Analysis</h3>\n  <p>Despite its mathematical power, dimensional analysis possesses strict operational limitations that students must master for board examinations:</p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">1. Inability to Determine Dimensionless Constants:</b> Dimensional analysis cannot evaluate the numerical value of dimensionless proportionality constants (k, 2pi, ½). These can only be established through physical experimentation or rigorous calculus derivations.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">2. Breakdown Beyond Three Variables in Mechanics:</b> In mechanics, we only have 3 base equations corresponding to [M], [L], and [T]. If a physical quantity depends upon more than three independent physical quantities, we obtain more unknowns than equations, rendering the system insoluble via dimensional analysis alone.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">3. Inapplicable to Transcendental Functions:</b> Trigonometric (sin \theta, cos omega t), logarithmic (ln x), and exponential (e<sup>-kx</sup>) functions are pure dimensionless quantities. If an equation incorporates these functions, dimensional analysis cannot derive the relationship.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">4. Insensitivity to Directionality (Scalar vs Vector Ambiguity):</b> Two radically different physical concepts may share identical dimensions. For example, <b>Work</b> (a scalar) and <b>Torque</b> (a rotational vector) both possess dimensional formula [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]. Dimensional analysis cannot distinguish whether a quantity is scalar or vector.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF512F;\">5. Failure on Multi-Term Additive Equations:</b> Dimensional analysis cannot derive formulas containing additive terms on one side, such as s = ut + ½ at<sup>2</sup> or v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as. It can only test their dimensional consistency.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- MASTER REVISION FORMULA CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Quick Reference Formulas & Constants &bull; Unit I: Units and Measurements</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Fundamental Measurement Invariance:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Q = n_1 u_1 = n_2 u_2<br>\n          n propto <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u</span></span>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Supplementary Angles:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Plane Angle: d\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> \text{ rad}<br>\n          Solid Angle: dOmega = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dA</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> \text{ sr}\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Unit Conversion Master Equation:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          n_2 = n_1 left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M_1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M_2</span></span>\right]<sup>a</sup> left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L_1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L_2</span></span>\right]<sup>b</sup> left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T_1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T_2</span></span>\right]<sup>c</sup>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Standard Unit Factors:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          1 \text{ J} = 10<sup>7</sup> \text{ Erg} &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1 \text{ N} = 10<sup>5</sup> \text{ Dyne}<br>\n          1 \text{ AU} = 1.496 \times 10<sup>11</sup> \text{ m}<br>\n          1 \text{ ly} = 9.46 \times 10<sup>15</sup> \text{ m} &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1 \text{ pc} = 3.08 \times 10<sup>16</sup> \text{ m}\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Critical Dimensional Constants:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Gravitational G = [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n          Planck h = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<br>\n          Permittivity \u000barepsilon_0 = [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>]<br>\n          Permeability mu_0 = [M L T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Identical Dimensional Pairs:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Work &bull; Energy &bull; Torque: [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n          Impulse &bull; Momentum: [M L T<sup>-1</sup>]<br>\n          Pressure &bull; Stress &bull; Modulus: [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n          Surface Tension &bull; Spring Const: [M T<sup>-2</sup>]$\n        </span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

export const c11Phy1HtmlSolutions = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      NCERT & Master Solutions Guide (100 Questions)\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 11 Physics Unit I: Physical World and Measurement &bull; Units and Measurements (05 Marks)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: VSA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark each) &bull; Q1 to Q40\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Fundamental definitions, 1-line physical reasoning, SI base and supplementary units, and basic dimensions.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q1: Define a physical quantity with two examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A physical quantity is any characteristic or property of an object, substance, or phenomenon that can be measured using an appropriate scientific instrument and quantified in terms of a numerical value and a unit. <b>Examples:</b> Mass (kg), Velocity (m s<sup>-1</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q2: State the fundamental equation of physical measurement.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The fundamental equation of measurement is <b>Q = n &times; u</b>, where <i>Q</i> is the physical quantity, <i>n</i> is the numerical magnitude, and <i>u</i> is the chosen standard unit. Since <i>Q</i> is constant, <b>n<sub>1</sub> u<sub>1</sub> = n<sub>2</sub> u<sub>2</sub> &rArr; n &prop; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q3: Why is a standard unit required in scientific measurement?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A standard unit is required to provide an invariable, reproducible, and universally accepted reference of known magnitude, eliminating subjective human sensory errors and ensuring consistent scientific communication worldwide.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q4: Name the three historical metric/imperial systems of units.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The three historical systems are: (1) <b>CGS System</b> (centimeter, gram, second), (2) <b>FPS System</b> (foot, pound, second), and (3) <b>MKS System</b> (meter, kilogram, second).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q5: What does the abbreviation 'SI' stand for, and in which year was it adopted?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The abbreviation 'SI' stands for <b>Syst&egrave;me International d'Unit&eacute;s</b> (International System of Units). It was formally adopted in <b>1971</b> by the 14th General Conference on Weights and Measures (CGPM).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q6: List all seven fundamental base quantities in the SI system.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The seven fundamental base quantities are: (1) Length (meter, m), (2) Mass (kilogram, kg), (3) Time (second, s), (4) Electric Current (ampere, A), (5) Thermodynamic Temperature (kelvin, K), (6) Amount of Substance (mole, mol), and (7) Luminous Intensity (candela, cd).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q7: Define fundamental (base) units.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Fundamental units are the basic, mutually independent physical units that cannot be expressed in terms of any other units, nor can they be derived from each other. They form the primary foundation of a system of units.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q8: Define derived units with two examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Derived units are units of measurement expressed algebraically as products, quotients, or powers of fundamental base units. <b>Examples:</b> Velocity (m s<sup>-1</sup>), Force (Newton, 1 N = 1 kg m s<sup>-2</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q9: Name the two supplementary units of the SI system along with their symbols.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The two supplementary units are: (1) <b>Radian (rad)</b> for plane angle, and (2) <b>Steradian (sr)</b> for solid angle.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q10: Can a physical quantity have a unit but no dimensions? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>Yes.</b> Both <b>plane angle</b> (measured in radians, rad) and <b>solid angle</b> (measured in steradians, sr) possess recognized SI units, but their dimensional formulas are strictly dimensionless: [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q11: Can a physical quantity have dimensions but no unit? Explain.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>No.</b> Any physical quantity that possesses non-zero dimensions must inevitably be expressible in terms of fundamental base units, and therefore it must have a unit.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q12: Define one radian (1 rad).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      One radian is the plane angle subtended at the center of a circle by a circular arc whose length is exactly equal to the radius of the circle: &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">s</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = 1 rad.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q13: Define one steradian (1 sr).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      One steradian is the solid angle subtended at the center of a sphere by a spherical surface patch whose area is equal to the square of the sphere's radius: &Omega; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> = 1 sr.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q14: What is the total solid angle subtended by a complete sphere at its center?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The total surface area of a sphere is A = 4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>. Therefore, the total solid angle subtended at the center is &Omega;<sub>total</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; r<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <b>4&pi; steradians (4&pi; sr)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q15: Why is the SI system called a 'coherent' system of units?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The SI system is called coherent because all derived units can be obtained purely by algebraic multiplication and division of the base and supplementary units without introducing any arbitrary numerical conversion factors.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q16: Why is the SI system termed a 'rational' system of units?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The SI system is rational because it assigns only one unique unit to any given physical quantity across all scientific disciplines. For example, all forms of energy (mechanical, heat, chemical, electrical) are expressed exclusively in <b>Joules (J)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q17: Define one Astronomical Unit (AU) and state its value in meters.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      One Astronomical Unit (AU) is the mean orbital distance between the center of the Earth and the center of the Sun. Its value is <b>1 AU = 1.496 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> m</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q18: Define one Light Year (ly) and state its value in meters.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      One Light Year (ly) is the total distance traversed by light in vacuum in one Julian year (365.25 days). Its value is: 1 ly = c &times; t = (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>) &times; (365.25 &times; 86400 s) = <b>9.46 &times; 10<sup>15</sup> m</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q19: Define one Parsec (pc) and state its relation to light years.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      One Parsec (parallactic second) is the distance at which an astronomical baseline of 1 AU subtends a parallax angle of 1 arcsecond (1''). Its value is <b>1 pc = 3.08 &times; 10<sup>16</sup> m &approx; 3.26 light years</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q20: Arrange the astronomical distance units AU, light year, and parsec in increasing order.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      In increasing order of magnitude: <b>1 AU &lt; 1 Light Year &lt; 1 Parsec</b> (1.496 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> m &lt; 9.46 &times; 10<sup>15</sup> m &lt; 3.08 &times; 10<sup>16</sup> m).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q21: Define one Fermi (Femtometer) and state where it is used.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      One Fermi is defined as <b>1 fm = 10<sup>-15</sup> m</b>. It is universally used in nuclear physics to measure the radii and cross-sections of atomic nuclei.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q22: Define one Angstrom (Å) and write its value in meters and nanometers.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      One Angstrom is defined as <b>1 &Aring; = 10<sup>-10</sup> m = 0.1 nm</b>. It is commonly employed to express atomic radii, chemical bond lengths, and wavelengths of visible light.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q23: What is the Chandrasekhar Limit (CSL)? State its numerical value.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The Chandrasekhar Limit is the practical largest unit of mass in astrophysics, representing the maximum stable mass of a white dwarf star: <b>1 CSL = 1.4 &times; M<sub>Sun</sub> &approx; 2.8 &times; 10<sup>30</sup> kg</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q24: What is meant by 'significant figures' in experimental measurement?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Significant figures are the total number of digits in an experimental measurement that are known reliably and with certainty, plus the first single terminal digit that carries experimental uncertainty.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q25: State the number of significant figures in 0.000450 m.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      There are <b>3 significant figures</b> (4, 5, 0). The initial leading zeros (0.000) merely indicate the decimal position and are non-significant, while the terminal trailing zero after the decimal is significant.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q26: State the number of significant figures in 3.080 × 10¹¹ m.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      There are <b>4 significant figures</b> (3, 0, 8, 0). The exponential power of 10 does not influence the count of significant figures.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q27: Round off 7.835 to 3 significant figures.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      By the even-odd rule, the digit to drop is 5 preceded by the odd digit 3. Therefore, 3 is increased by 1 to make it even: <b>7.84</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q28: Round off 12.6450 to 4 significant figures.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The digit to drop is 5 followed by zeros, preceded by the even digit 4. By the even-odd rule, the even digit remains unchanged: <b>12.64</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q29: State the governing rule for significant figures in addition and subtraction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The final sum or difference must be rounded off to retain only as many <b>decimal places</b> as are present in the measurement with the least number of decimal places.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q30: State the governing rule for significant figures in multiplication and division.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The final product or quotient must be rounded off to retain only as many <b>significant figures</b> as are present in the original measurement with the least number of significant figures.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q31: Define the dimensions of a physical quantity.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The dimensions of a physical quantity are the powers (or exponents) to which the fundamental base units [M], [L], [T], etc., must be raised to represent derived units of that physical quantity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q32: What is a dimensional equation? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A dimensional equation is an algebraic equation equating a physical quantity to its dimensional formula. <b>Example:</b> Force equation: <b>[F] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q33: Write the dimensional formula and SI unit of Universal Gravitational Constant (G).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      From F = G <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m<sub>1</sub> m<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span>, G = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F r<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m<sub>1</sub> m<sub>2</sub></span></span>. Dimensional formula: <b>[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>. SI unit: <b>N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q34: Write the dimensional formula and SI unit of Planck's Constant (h).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      From E = h &nu;, h = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&nu;</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[T<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>. Dimensional formula: <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</b>. SI unit: <b>J s</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q35: Which physical quantity has the same dimensions as Planck's constant?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>Angular Momentum (L = m v r)</b> has the dimensional formula [M][L T<sup>-1</sup>][L] = <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</b>, which is strictly identical to Planck's constant (h).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q36: Name two pairs of physical quantities that share identical dimensions.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      (1) <b>Work and Torque:</b> Both possess dimensions <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.<br>(2) <b>Impulse and Linear Momentum:</b> Both possess dimensions <b>[M L T<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q37: Define a dimensional constant and give two examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A dimensional constant is a physical constant that has a fixed numerical value and also carries dimensions. <b>Examples:</b> Universal Gravitational Constant G ([M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]), Planck's constant h ([M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q38: Define a dimensionless variable and give two examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A dimensionless variable is a physical parameter that can take variable numerical values but possesses zero dimensions ([M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]). <b>Examples:</b> Strain (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span></span>), Refractive index (&mu;).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q39: State the Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity states that an equation is dimensionally correct if and only if the dimensions of each and every term on both sides of the equation (separated by +, -, or =) are strictly identical.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q40: State two limitations of dimensional analysis.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      (1) It cannot determine the numerical value of dimensionless proportionality constants (e.g. 2&pi;, &frac12;).<br>(2) It fails when a physical quantity depends on trigonometric, logarithmic, or exponential functions.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 & 3 Marks each) &bull; Q41 to Q80\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Conceptual derivations, error propagation analysis, unit conversions, and dimensional consistency proofs.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q41: Distinguish between fundamental and derived units with two distinct points and examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Definition:</b> Fundamental units are independent and cannot be defined or derived from any other unit (e.g. meter for length, kilogram for mass). Derived units are expressed algebraically as products or quotients of fundamental units (e.g. m s<sup>-1</sup> for velocity, N for force).<br><b>2. Mutability & Count:</b> In the SI system, there are strictly 7 fundamental base units. In contrast, the number of derived units is virtually limitless, spanning all disciplines of science.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q42: Explain why plane angle is a dimensionless quantity despite having a well-defined unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Plane angle &theta; is defined geometrically as the ratio of arc length (s) to radius (r):<br><div style='text-align:center;margin:6px 0;'>&theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">s</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span></div>Both arc length s and radius r possess the dimensional formula of length [L]. Therefore:<br><div style='text-align:center;margin:6px 0;'>[&theta;] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L]</span></span> = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</div>Because all base dimensions cancel to zero powers, the quantity is completely dimensionless. However, to distinguish angular orientation from pure linear numbers, the SI assigns it the supplementary unit <b>radian (rad)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q43: Differentiate between accuracy and precision in scientific measurement.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>Accuracy</b> refers to how close a measured value is to the true, accepted standard value of the physical quantity. It depends primarily on the minimization of systematic errors.<br><b>Precision</b> refers to the degree of agreement or reproducibility among multiple repeated independent measurements of the same quantity under identical conditions. It depends directly on the least count and resolution of the measuring instrument, irrespective of how close the values are to the true value.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q44: A rectangular plate has length l = (16.2 ± 0.1) cm and breadth b = (10.1 ± 0.1) cm. Find its area and percentage uncertainty.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>Area:</b> A = l &times; b = 16.2 &times; 10.1 = <b>163.62 cm<sup>2</sup></b>.<br>Fractional uncertainty: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;l</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;b</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">b</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">16.2</span></span> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">10.1</span></span> = 0.00617 + 0.00990 = 0.01607.<br>Absolute uncertainty: &Delta;A = 0.01607 &times; 163.62 = <b>2.63 cm<sup>2</sup></b> &approx; 3 cm<sup>2</sup>.<br>Percentage uncertainty = 0.01607 &times; 100% = <b>1.61%</b>.<br>Rounding to significant figures: <b>Area = (164 &plusmn; 3) cm<sup>2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q45: Calculate the volume of a sphere of radius r = 2.14 cm, expressing the answer to appropriate significant figures.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Volume of a sphere: V = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3</span></span> &pi; r<sup>3</sup>.<br>Substitute r = 2.14 cm: V = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3</span></span> &times; 3.14159 &times; (2.14)<sup>3</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3</span></span> &times; 3.14159 &times; 9.80034 = <b>41.088 cm<sup>3</sup></b>.<br>Since the measured radius 2.14 cm has <b>3 significant figures</b>, the calculated volume must be rounded to 3 significant figures:<br><b>V = 41.1 cm<sup>3</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q46: Perform the arithmetic operation 436.32 g + 227.2 g + 0.301 g and express the result with proper significant figures.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Add the terms algebraically: 436.32 + 227.2 + 0.301 = <b>663.821 g</b>.<br>Rule for addition: The result must retain only as many decimal places as the term with the fewest decimal places.<br>&bull; 436.32 has 2 decimal places.<br>&bull; 227.2 has <b>1 decimal place</b> (least precise).<br>&bull; 0.301 has 3 decimal places.<br>Rounding 663.821 to 1 decimal place gives: <b>663.8 g</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q47: Mass of a substance is 5.74 g and its volume is 1.2 cm³. Calculate its density with proper significant figures.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Density &rho; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Mass</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Volume</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">5.74 g</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1.2 cm<sup>3</sup></span></span> = 4.78333... g cm<sup>-3</sup>.<br>Rule for division: The result must have the same number of significant figures as the least precise quantity.<br>&bull; Mass (5.74 g) has 3 significant figures.<br>&bull; Volume (1.2 cm<sup>3</sup>) has <b>2 significant figures</b>.<br>Rounding to 2 significant figures: <b>Density = 4.8 g cm<sup>-3</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q48: Convert 1 Newton of force into CGS unit Dyne using dimensional analysis.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Dimensional formula of Force: [F] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>] &rArr; a = 1, b = 1, c = -2.<br>Formula: n<sub>2</sub> = n<sub>1</sub> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>a</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>b</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>c</sup>.<br>Here SI (System 1): M<sub>1</sub> = 1 kg = 1000 g, L<sub>1</sub> = 1 m = 100 cm, T<sub>1</sub> = 1 s, n<sub>1</sub> = 1.<br>CGS (System 2): M<sub>2</sub> = 1 g, L<sub>2</sub> = 1 cm, T<sub>2</sub> = 1 s.<br>n<sub>2</sub> = 1 &times; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1000 g</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 g</span></span>]<sup>1</sup> &times; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">100 cm</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 cm</span></span>]<sup>1</sup> &times; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 s</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 s</span></span>]<sup>-2</sup> = 10<sup>3</sup> &times; 10<sup>2</sup> &times; 1 = <b>10<sup>5</sup></b>.<br>Thus: <b>1 Newton = 10<sup>5</sup> Dyne</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q49: Convert 1 Joule of energy into CGS unit Erg using dimensional analysis.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Dimensional formula of Energy: [E] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] &rArr; a = 1, b = 2, c = -2.<br>n<sub>2</sub> = n<sub>1</sub> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>a</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>b</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>c</sup>.<br>SI: M<sub>1</sub> = 1 kg = 10<sup>3</sup> g, L<sub>1</sub> = 1 m = 10<sup>2</sup> cm, T<sub>1</sub> = 1 s, n<sub>1</sub> = 1.<br>CGS: M<sub>2</sub> = 1 g, L<sub>2</sub> = 1 cm, T<sub>2</sub> = 1 s.<br>n<sub>2</sub> = 1 &times; (10<sup>3</sup>)<sup>1</sup> &times; (10<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> &times; (1)<sup>-2</sup> = 10<sup>3</sup> &times; 10<sup>4</sup> = <b>10<sup>7</sup></b>.<br>Therefore: <b>1 Joule = 10<sup>7</sup> Erg</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q50: The value of Universal Gravitational Constant is G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻² in SI. Convert it into CGS units.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Dimensions of G: [G] = [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] &rArr; a = -1, b = 3, c = -2.<br>Formula: n<sub>2</sub> = n<sub>1</sub> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>-1</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>3</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>-2</sup>.<br>n<sub>2</sub> = 6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> &times; (10<sup>3</sup>)<sup>-1</sup> &times; (10<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> &times; (1)<sup>-2</sup><br>n<sub>2</sub> = 6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> &times; 10<sup>6</sup> = <b>6.67 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> dyn cm<sup>2</sup> g<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q51: In van der Waals' gas equation (P + a/V²)(V - b) = RT, determine the dimensional formulas and SI units of constants a and b.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      By the Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity:<br>1. The term <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sup>2</sup></span></span> is added to pressure P, so it must have the same dimensions as pressure:<br>[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sup>2</sup></span></span>] = [P] &rArr; [a] = [P][V<sup>2</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>][(L<sup>3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>][L<sup>6</sup>] = <b>[M L<sup>5</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.<br>SI unit of a: <b>N m<sup>4</sup> = kg m<sup>5</sup> s<sup>-2</sup></b>.<br>2. The term b is subtracted from volume V, so it must have dimensions of volume:<br>[b] = [V] = <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.<br>SI unit of b: <b>m<sup>3</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q52: In the relation F = a√x + b t², where F is force, x is distance, and t is time, find the dimensional formulas of a and b.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      By Principle of Homogeneity, each term on RHS must equal the dimensions of Force [F] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]:<br>1. [a &radic;x] = [F] &rArr; [a][L<sup>1/2</sup>] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>] &rArr; [a] = [M L<sup>(1 - 1/2)</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] = <b>[M L<sup>1/2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.<br>2. [b t<sup>2</sup>] = [F] &rArr; [b][T<sup>2</sup>] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>] &rArr; [b] = [M L T<sup>-2 - 2</sup>] = <b>[M L T<sup>-4</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q53: Check the dimensional consistency of the third equation of motion: v² - u² = 2 a s.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      1. LHS: [v<sup>2</sup>] = ([L T<sup>-1</sup>])<sup>2</sup> = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>[u<sup>2</sup>] = ([L T<sup>-1</sup>])<sup>2</sup> = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>Difference of two identical velocity squares: [LHS] = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>2. RHS: The numerical factor 2 is dimensionless ([2] = [1]).<br>[2 a s] = [a][s] = [L T<sup>-2</sup>][L] = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>Since [LHS] = [RHS] = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>], the equation is <b>dimensionally correct and consistent</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q54: Check the dimensional validity of the expression for escape velocity: v_e = √(2 G M / R).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      1. LHS: [v<sub>e</sub>] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>].<br>2. RHS: Factor 2 is dimensionless. Dimensions inside square root:<br>[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span>] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>][M]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L]</span></span> = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>Taking square root: [&radic;(2GM/R)] = ([L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>])<sup>1/2</sup> = [L T<sup>-1</sup>].<br>Since [LHS] = [RHS] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>], the formula is <b>dimensionally consistent</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q55: Find the dimensions of (a / b) in the equation P = (a - t²) / (b x), where P is pressure, x is distance, and t is time.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      1. In the numerator (a - t<sup>2</sup>), a is subtracted from t<sup>2</sup>, so by homogeneity: [a] = [t<sup>2</sup>] = <b>[T<sup>2</sup>]</b>.<br>2. Now [P] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[a - t<sup>2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[b x]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[T<sup>2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[b][L]</span></span>.<br>Since [P] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]:<br>[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[T<sup>2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[b][L]</span></span> &rArr; [b] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[T<sup>2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>][L]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[T<sup>2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M T<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span> = <b>[M<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>4</sup>]</b>.<br>3. Ratio [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">b</span></span>] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[T<sup>2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>4</sup>]</span></span> = <b>[M L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q56: Explain why dimensional analysis cannot derive the exact formula s = u t + ½ a t².</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Dimensional analysis uses the power-law product approach (Q = k A<sup>a</sup> B<sup>b</sup> C<sup>c</sup>). It assumes that the dependent quantity is a single product term of variables. It cannot determine the plus (+) or minus (-) signs separating independent additive components, nor can it determine multiple independent numerical coefficients (1 for ut and &frac12; for at<sup>2</sup>). It can only verify the consistency of each term individually.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q57: If Planck's constant (h), speed of light in vacuum (c), and Newton's gravitational constant (G) are chosen as fundamental quantities, find the dimensions of Length.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Let Length L &prop; h<sup>a</sup> c<sup>b</sup> G<sup>c</sup>.<br>Substitute dimensions: [L] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>a</sup> [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>b</sup> [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>c</sup><br>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>] = [M<sup>(a - c)</sup> L<sup>(2a + b + 3c)</sup> T<sup>(-a - b - 2c)</sup>].<br>Equating powers:<br>1. a - c = 0 &rArr; a = c.<br>2. -a - b - 2c = 0 &rArr; -3a - b = 0 &rArr; b = -3a.<br>3. 2a + b + 3c = 1 &rArr; 2a - 3a + 3a = 1 &rArr; 2a = 1 &rArr; a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>, c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>, b = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>Therefore: <b>Length = [h<sup>1/2</sup> G<sup>1/2</sup> c<sup>-3/2</sup>] = &radic;(h G / c<sup>3</sup>)</b> (Planck length &approx; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-35</sup> m).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q58: Using the same fundamental set (h, c, G), find the dimensions of Mass.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Let Mass M &prop; h<sup>a</sup> c<sup>b</sup> G<sup>c</sup>.<br>[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>] = [M<sup>(a - c)</sup> L<sup>(2a + b + 3c)</sup> T<sup>(-a - b - 2c)</sup>].<br>1. a - c = 1 &rArr; c = a - 1.<br>2. -a - b - 2(a - 1) = 0 &rArr; -3a - b + 2 = 0 &rArr; b = 2 - 3a.<br>3. 2a + (2 - 3a) + 3(a - 1) = 0 &rArr; 2a - 1 = 0 &rArr; a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>Then c = &frac12; - 1 = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>, and b = 2 - 3(&frac12;) = +<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>Therefore: <b>Mass = [h<sup>1/2</sup> c<sup>1/2</sup> G<sup>-1/2</sup>] = &radic;(h c / G)</b> (Planck mass &approx; 2.18 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> kg).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q59: Using the same fundamental set (h, c, G), find the dimensions of Time.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Time t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Length</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(h G / c<sup>3</sup>)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span> = <b>&radic;(h G / c<sup>5</sup>) = [h<sup>1/2</sup> G<sup>1/2</sup> c<sup>-5/2</sup>]</b> (Planck time &approx; 5.39 &times; 10<sup>-44</sup> s).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q60: Deduce by dimensional analysis an expression for the viscous drag force F acting on a spherical ball of radius r moving with velocity v through a fluid of viscosity η.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Assume F = k &eta;<sup>a</sup> r<sup>b</sup> v<sup>c</sup>.<br>Substitute dimensions: [M L T<sup>-2</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>a</sup> [L]<sup>b</sup> [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>(-a + b + c)</sup> T<sup>(-a - c)</sup>].<br>Equating powers:<br>&bull; [M]: a = 1.<br>&bull; [T]: -a - c = -2 &rArr; -1 - c = -2 &rArr; c = 1.<br>&bull; [L]: -a + b + c = 1 &rArr; -1 + b + 1 = 1 &rArr; b = 1.<br>Substitute a = 1, b = 1, c = 1: <b>F = k &eta; r v</b>. Experimentally k = 6&pi;, yielding Stokes' Law: <b>F = 6&pi; &eta; r v</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q61: Deduce an expression for the frequency (ν) of vibration of a stretched string depending on its length (l), tension (T), and mass per unit length (m).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Let &nu; = k l<sup>a</sup> T<sup>b</sup> m<sup>c</sup>.<br>Dimensions: [&nu;] = [T<sup>-1</sup>], [l] = [L], [Tension T] = [Force] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>], [m] = Mass/Length = [M L<sup>-1</sup>].<br>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>] = [L]<sup>a</sup> [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>b</sup> [M L<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>(b + c)</sup> L<sup>(a + b - c)</sup> T<sup>-2b</sup>].<br>1. -2b = -1 &rArr; b = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>2. b + c = 0 &rArr; c = -b = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>3. a + b - c = 0 &rArr; a + &frac12; - (-&frac12;) = 0 &rArr; a + 1 = 0 &rArr; a = -1.<br>Thus: &nu; = k l<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>1/2</sup> m<sup>-1/2</sup> = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span> &radic;(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m</span></span>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q62: A calorie is a unit of heat equal to 4.2 J, where 1 J = 1 kg m² s⁻². If a new unit of mass is α kg, unit of length is β m, and unit of time is γ s, find the magnitude of calorie in the new system.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Energy dimensions: [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] &rArr; a = 1, b = 2, c = -2.<br>n<sub>1</sub> = 4.2.<br>SI: M<sub>1</sub> = 1 kg, L<sub>1</sub> = 1 m, T<sub>1</sub> = 1 s.<br>New system: M<sub>2</sub> = &alpha; kg, L<sub>2</sub> = &beta; m, T<sub>2</sub> = &gamma; s.<br>Formula: n<sub>2</sub> = n<sub>1</sub> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>a</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>b</sup> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>2</sub></span></span>]<sup>c</sup><br>n<sub>2</sub> = 4.2 &times; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 kg</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&alpha; kg</span></span>]<sup>1</sup> &times; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&beta; m</span></span>]<sup>2</sup> &times; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 s</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&gamma; s</span></span>]<sup>-2</sup><br><b>n<sub>2</sub> = 4.2 &alpha;<sup>-1</sup> &beta;<sup>-2</sup> &gamma;<sup>2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q63: Find the dimensions of Linear Mass Density, Surface Mass Density, and Volumetric Mass Density.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      1. <b>Linear Mass Density (&lambda; = M / L):</b> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span></span>] = <b>[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.<br>2. <b>Surface Mass Density (&sigma; = M / A):</b> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sup>2</sup></span></span>] = <b>[M L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.<br>3. <b>Volumetric Mass Density (&rho; = M / V):</b> [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sup>3</sup></span></span>] = <b>[M L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q64: Write the dimensional formula and SI unit of Electric Permittivity of Free Space (ε₀).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      From Coulomb's Law F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> &rArr; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; F r<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br>Dimensions: [&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">([A T])<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L T<sup>-2</sup>][L<sup>2</sup>]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[A<sup>2</sup> T<sup>2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span> = <b>[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>]</b>.<br>SI unit: <b>C<sup>2</sup> N<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-2</sup> or Farad per meter (F m<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q65: Write the dimensional formula and SI unit of Magnetic Permeability of Free Space (μ₀).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      From Biot-Savart Law or force per unit length between parallel currents: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dF</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dL</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub> I<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; r</span></span> &rArr; &mu;<sub>0</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; r (dF/dL)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br>Dimensions: [&mu;<sub>0</sub>] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L] [M L T<sup>-2</sup> / L]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[A<sup>2</sup>]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L T<sup>-2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[A<sup>2</sup>]</span></span> = <b>[M L T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.<br>SI unit: <b>Tesla-meter per Ampere (T m A<sup>-1</sup>) or Henry per meter (H m<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q66: Show that the product (ε₀ μ₀) has dimensions [L⁻² T²] and relates to the speed of light.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Multiply their dimensional formulas:<br>[&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &mu;<sub>0</sub>] = [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>] &times; [M L T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>] = [M<sup>(-1+1)} L<sup>(-3+1)} T<sup>(4-2)} A<sup>(2-2)}] = <b>[L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>2</sup>]</b>.<br>Since speed of light c has dimensions [L T<sup>-1</sup>], c<sup>2</sup> has dimensions [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>Therefore: [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span>] = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] = [c<sup>2</sup>] &rArr; <b>c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &mu;<sub>0</sub>)</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q67: Find the dimensions of Resistance (R) and Capacitance (C), and show that RC has dimensions of Time.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      1. <b>Resistance R = V / I:</b> [V] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>] &rArr; [R] = <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.<br>2. <b>Capacitance C = Q / V:</b> [Q] = [A T] &rArr; [C] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[A T]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span> = <b>[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>]</b>.<br>3. <b>Product RC (Capacitive Time Constant):</b><br>[RC] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>] &times; [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>] = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>(-3+4)} A<sup>0</sup>] = <b>[T]</b>. Hence RC represents time.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q68: Find the dimensions of Inductance (L), and show that L/R has dimensions of Time.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      1. Self-induced EMF &epsilon; = -L <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dI</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> &rArr; [L] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[&epsilon;][dt]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[dI]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>][T]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[A]</span></span> = <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.<br>2. Ratio L / R (Inductive Time Constant):<br>[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span>] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span> = [T<sup>-2 - (-3)} ] = <b>[T]</b>. Hence L/R has the dimension of time.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q69: Show that √(L C) has dimensions of Time.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      [LC] = [L][C] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>] &times; [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>] = [T<sup>2</sup>].<br>Taking the square root: [&radic;(LC)] = ([T<sup>2</sup>])<sup>1/2</sup> = <b>[T]</b>. This represents the resonant period of an LC circuit (T = 2&pi;&radic;(LC)).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q70: The velocity of sound in a gas depends on its pressure (P) and density (ρ). Derive the formula for sound velocity dimensionally.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Assume v = k P<sup>a</sup> &rho;<sup>b</sup>.<br>[L T<sup>-1</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>a</sup> [M L<sup>-3</sup>]<sup>b</sup> = [M<sup>(a + b)</sup> L<sup>(-a - 3b)</sup> T<sup>-2a</sup>].<br>1. -2a = -1 &rArr; a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>2. a + b = 0 &rArr; b = -a = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>3. Check length: -a - 3b = -&frac12; - 3(-&frac12;) = -&frac12; + 1.5 = 1 (Verified).<br>Therefore: v = k P<sup>1/2</sup> &rho;<sup>-1/2</sup> = <b>k &radic;(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&rho;</span></span>)</b> (Newton-Laplace formula: v = &radic;(&gamma;P/&rho;)).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q71: A body of mass m is suspended from a spring of force constant k. Deduce its frequency of oscillation dimensionally.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Frequency &nu; depends on mass m and spring constant k (Force/length = [M T<sup>-2</sup>]).<br>Let &nu; = c m<sup>a</sup> k<sup>b</sup>.<br>[T<sup>-1</sup>] = [M]<sup>a</sup> [M T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>b</sup> = [M<sup>(a + b)</sup> T<sup>-2b</sup>].<br>1. -2b = -1 &rArr; b = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>2. a + b = 0 &rArr; a = -b = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>Thus: &nu; = c m<sup>-1/2</sup> k<sup>1/2</sup> = <b>c &radic;(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m</span></span>)</b>. (Exact solution: &nu; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi;</span></span> &radic;(k/m)).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q72: The critical velocity v_c of a liquid flowing through a tube depends on viscosity η, density ρ, and tube diameter D. Derive the relationship.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Let v<sub>c</sub> = k &eta;<sup>a</sup> &rho;<sup>b</sup> D<sup>c</sup>.<br>[L T<sup>-1</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>a</sup> [M L<sup>-3</sup>]<sup>b</sup> [L]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>(a + b)</sup> L<sup>(-a - 3b + c)</sup> T<sup>-a</sup>].<br>1. -a = -1 &rArr; a = 1.<br>2. a + b = 0 &rArr; b = -1.<br>3. -a - 3b + c = 1 &rArr; -1 - 3(-1) + c = 1 &rArr; -1 + 3 + c = 1 &rArr; c = -1.<br>Therefore: v<sub>c</sub> = k <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&eta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&rho; D</span></span> = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>R</sub> &eta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&rho; D</span></span></b>, where N<sub>R</sub> is Reynolds' number.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q73: State whether the following statement is true or false with reasoning: 'A dimensionally correct equation is always physically correct.'</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>FALSE.</b> An equation may be dimensionally homogeneous but physically wrong due to missing dimensionless constants or erroneous coefficients. For instance, the equation s = 2 u t + 7 a t<sup>2</sup> is perfectly dimensionally consistent ([L] = [L] = [L]), but it is physically incorrect because the true physical coefficients are 1 and &frac12;.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q74: State whether the following statement is true or false with reasoning: 'A physically correct equation is always dimensionally correct.'</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>TRUE.</b> All genuine, valid physical laws describe real relations among measurable physical attributes. By the Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity, terms representing different physical dimensions cannot be added or equated. Therefore, every physically valid equation must strictly be dimensionally homogeneous.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q75: If displacement of a particle is given by y = A sin(ω t - k x), find the dimensional formulas of ω and k.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      The argument of a trigonometric function is an angle &theta;, which is strictly <b>dimensionless</b> ([M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>] = [1]).<br>1. [&omega; t] = [1] &rArr; [&omega;] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[t]</span></span> = <b>[T<sup>-1</sup>]</b> (SI unit: rad s<sup>-1</sup>).<br>2. [k x] = [1] &rArr; [k] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[x]</span></span> = <b>[L<sup>-1</sup>]</b> (Propagation constant, SI unit: rad m<sup>-1</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q76: In the equation y = A sin(ω t) + B cos(k x), state the dimensions of A and B if y is in meters.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Since the trigonometric terms sin(&omega;t) and cos(kx) are dimensionless pure numbers ([1]), the terms A and B must directly share the dimensions of displacement y:<br><b>[A] = [B] = [y] = [L] = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q77: Find the dimensional formula of Thermal Conductivity (K).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Rate of heat conduction: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Q</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">t</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">K A (T<sub>1</sub> - T<sub>2</sub>)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span></span> &rArr; K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Q d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A (T<sub>1</sub> - T<sub>2</sub>) t</span></span>.<br>Dimensions: [K] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>][L]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L<sup>2</sup>][&Theta;][T]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L<sup>2</sup> T &Theta;]</span></span> = <b>[M L T<sup>-3</sup> &Theta;<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.<br>SI unit: <b>W m<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q78: Find the dimensional formula of Stefan's Constant (σ).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      From Stefan-Boltzmann Law: E = &sigma; T<sup>4</sup>, where E is radiant energy emitted per unit area per unit second: [E] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[Energy]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[Area][Time]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L<sup>2</sup>][T]</span></span> = [M T<sup>-3</sup>].<br>&sigma; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sup>4</sup></span></span> &rArr; [&sigma;] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M T<sup>-3</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[&Theta;<sup>4</sup>]</span></span> = <b>[M L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> &Theta;<sup>-4</sup>]</b>.<br>SI unit: <b>W m<sup>-2</sup> K<sup>-4</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q79: Find the dimensional formula of Wien's Displacement Constant (b).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      From Wien's Displacement Law: &lambda;<sub>max</sub> &times; T = b.<br>Dimensions: [b] = [&lambda;<sub>max</sub>][T] = <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup> &Theta;<sup>1</sup>] = [L &Theta;]</b>.<br>SI unit: <b>meter-Kelvin (m K)</b>. Value &approx; 2.898 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> m K.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q80: Find the dimensional formula of Solar Constant (S).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Solar constant S is the solar radiant energy received normally per unit area per second at the outer boundary of Earth's atmosphere:<br>[S] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[Energy]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[Area][Time]</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">[L<sup>2</sup>][T]</span></span> = <b>[M L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-3</sup>]</b>.<br>SI unit: <b>W m<sup>-2</sup> or J s<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section C: Long Answer (LA) Questions (5 Marks each) &bull; Q81 to Q100\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Comprehensive multi-step board derivations, dimensional deducing of universal formulas, and master chapter synthesis.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q81: Explain the Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity. Using this principle, derive the formula for the time period (T) of a simple pendulum, detailing every algebraic step and justifying why bob mass does not appear in the final relation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity:</b> The Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity states that any physically meaningful equation must possess identical dimensions across every term on both sides. Terms with dissimilar dimensions cannot be added, subtracted, or equated.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Mathematical Derivation of Simple Pendulum Period (T):</b><br>\n      Assume time period T depends upon:<br>\n      &bull; Mass of the bob m raised to power a: m<sup>a</sup><br>\n      &bull; Effective length of pendulum l raised to power b: l<sup>b</sup><br>\n      &bull; Acceleration due to gravity g raised to power c: g<sup>c</sup><br>\n      Therefore: <b>T = k &middot; m<sup>a</sup> &middot; l<sup>b</sup> &middot; g<sup>c</sup></b>, where k is a dimensionless proportionality constant.<br><br>\n      <b>Step 1 &bull; Dimensional Representation:</b><br>\n      [LHS] = [T] = [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>1</sup>]<br>\n      [RHS] = [m]<sup>a</sup> [l]<sup>b</sup> [g]<sup>c</sup> = [M]<sup>a</sup> [L]<sup>b</sup> [L T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b+c</sup> T<sup>-2c</sup>].<br><br>\n      <b>Step 2 &bull; Equating Exponents:</b><br>\n      By Principle of Homogeneity, equate powers of base quantities:<br>\n      &bull; For [M]: <b>a = 0</b><br>\n      &bull; For [T]: -2c = 1 implies mathbf{c = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>}<br>\n      &bull; For [L]: b + c = 0 implies b = -c implies mathbf{b = +<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>}<br><br>\n      <b>Step 3 &bull; Formula Assembly:</b><br>\n      Substitute a = 0, b = 1/2, c = -1/2 back into the assumed relation:<br>\n      T = k cdot m<sup>0</sup> cdot l<sup>1/2</sup> cdot g<sup>-1/2</sup> = k sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>}.<br>\n      Experimental calibration establishes k = 2pi, yielding:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T = 2pi sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>}\n      </div>\n      <b>Physical Justification:</b> Since the exponent of mass is identically zero (a = 0), the period of a simple pendulum is strictly independent of the mass or composition of the bob. This is because inertial mass (m_i resisting acceleration) and gravitational mass (m_g experiencing gravitational pull) are equivalent, causing both light and heavy bobs to accelerate at identical rates (g).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q82: Derive an expression for the Centripetal Force (F) acting on an object of mass m moving with uniform speed v in a circular path of radius r, utilizing dimensional analysis. Discuss the physical significance of each exponent.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Hypothesis Setup:</b><br>\n      Centripetal force F keeps a body on a curved circular trajectory. We hypothesize that F depends upon:<br>\n      &bull; Mass of the body (m) to power a: m<sup>a</sup><br>\n      &bull; Orbital velocity (v) to power b: v<sup>b</sup><br>\n      &bull; Radius of curvature (r) to power c: r<sup>c</sup><br>\n      Relationship: <b>F = k &middot; m<sup>a</sup> &middot; v<sup>b</sup> &middot; r<sup>c</sup></b>, where k is a dimensionless constant.<br><br>\n      <b>Step 1 &bull; Dimensions of Individual Parameters:</b><br>\n      Force: [F] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      Mass: [m] = [M]<br>\n      Velocity: [v] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<br>\n      Radius: [r] = [L]<br><br>\n      <b>Step 2 &bull; Substitute Dimensions:</b><br>\n      [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] = [M]<sup>a</sup> [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>b</sup> [L]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>(b+c)</sup> T<sup>-b</sup>].<br><br>\n      <b>Step 3 &bull; Solve Linear Equations for Exponents:</b><br>\n      &bull; Comparing power of [M]: mathbf{a = 1}<br>\n      &bull; Comparing power of [T]: -b = -2 implies mathbf{b = 2}<br>\n      &bull; Comparing power of [L]: b + c = 1 implies 2 + c = 1 implies mathbf{c = -1}<br><br>\n      <b>Step 4 &bull; Synthesis of the Law:</b><br>\n      Substitute exponents into the original equation:<br>\n      F = k cdot m<sup>1</sup> cdot v<sup>2</sup> cdot r<sup>-1</sup> = k <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span>.<br>\n      In SI units, absolute definition of force yields k = 1, giving:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span>\n      </div>\n      <b>Physical Significance of Exponents:</b><br>\n      &bull; a = 1: Force scales linearly with mass (Newton's second law).<br>\n      &bull; b = 2: Doubling vehicle speed quadruples the required inward centripetal force, explaining sharp turn skid hazards.<br>\n      &bull; c = -1: Tighter curves (smaller r) require inversely greater centripetal force to avoid overturning.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q83: State Stokes' Law for viscous drag. Derive it by dimensional analysis, and explain the physical assumptions underlying this equation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Stokes' Law Statement:</b> When a smooth, rigid spherical body moves through a continuous, infinite, viscous fluid medium, the retarding viscous drag force F experienced by the sphere is directly proportional to its radius (r), its velocity (v), and the dynamic viscosity (&eta;) of the fluid.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Dimensional Derivation:</b><br>\n      Assume: <b>F = k &middot; &eta;<sup>a</sup> &middot; r<sup>b</sup> &middot; v<sup>c</sup></b> (where k is a dimensionless proportionality constant).<br>\n      Dimensions of parameters:<br>\n      &bull; Force [F] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      &bull; Viscosity [eta] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>] (from F = eta A \frac{dv}{dx})<br>\n      &bull; Radius [r] = [L]<br>\n      &bull; Velocity [v] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<br><br>\n      Substitute into power relation:<br>\n      [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>a</sup> [L]<sup>b</sup> [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>(-a + b + c)</sup> T<sup>(-a - c)</sup>].<br><br>\n      Equating powers of base quantities:<br>\n      &bull; For [M]: mathbf{a = 1}<br>\n      &bull; For [T]: -a - c = -2 implies -1 - c = -2 implies mathbf{c = 1}<br>\n      &bull; For [L]: -a + b + c = 1 implies -1 + b + 1 = 1 implies mathbf{b = 1}<br><br>\n      Substitute a = 1, b = 1, c = 1:<br>\n      F = k cdot eta cdot r cdot v.<br>\n      Sir George Gabriel Stokes proved analytically via hydrodynamic Navier-Stokes equations that k = 6pi, yielding:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        F = 6pi eta r v\n      </div>\n      <b>Physical Assumptions:</b><br>\n      (1) The fluid medium is infinite in extent.<br>\n      (2) The falling sphere is perfectly rigid, smooth, and spherical.<br>\n      (3) Velocity of sphere is small (laminar streamline flow, no turbulent eddies, low Reynolds number N_R < 1).<br>\n      (4) There is no slip between fluid and sphere surface.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q84: Deduce Poiseuille's formula for the rate of steady laminar volume flow (V) of a liquid through a narrow capillary tube of radius r and length l under a pressure head P, using dimensional analysis.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Hypothesis:</b><br>\n      Let volume of liquid flowing out per unit second (V = \text{Volume}/\text{time}, dimensions [L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]) depend on:<br>\n      (1) Pressure gradient along tube: P/l raised to power a<br>\n      (2) Radius of capillary tube: r raised to power b<br>\n      (3) Coefficient of viscosity of liquid: &eta; raised to power c<br>\n      Equation: <b>V = k &middot; (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span>)<sup>a</sup> &middot; r<sup>b</sup> &middot; &eta;<sup>c</sup></b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Dimensional Formulas:</b><br>\n      &bull; Rate of volume flow: [V] = [L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<br>\n      &bull; Pressure per unit length: [P/l] = \frac{[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}{[L]} = [M L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      &bull; Radius: [r] = [L]<br>\n      &bull; Viscosity: [eta] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<br><br>\n      <b>3. Equate Dimensions:</b><br>\n      [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>] = [M L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>a</sup> [L]<sup>b</sup> [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>(a + c)</sup> L<sup>(-2a + b - c)</sup> T<sup>(-2a - c)</sup>].<br><br>\n      Equate powers:<br>\n      &bull; [M]: a + c = 0 implies c = -a<br>\n      &bull; [T]: -2a - c = -1 implies -2a - (-a) = -1 implies -a = -1 implies mathbf{a = 1}<br>\n      Hence: mathbf{c = -1}<br>\n      &bull; [L]: -2a + b - c = 3 implies -2(1) + b - (-1) = 3 implies -2 + b + 1 = 3 implies b - 1 = 3 implies mathbf{b = 4}<br><br>\n      <b>4. Formula Synthesis:</b><br>\n      Substitute a = 1, b = 4, c = -1:<br>\n      V = k (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span>)<sup>1</sup> r<sup>4</sup> eta<sup>-1</sup> = k <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P r<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&eta; l</span></span>.<br>\n      Analytical fluid dynamics proves k = pi / 8, yielding <b>Poiseuille's Law</b>:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        V = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi; P r<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8 &eta; l</span></span>\n      </div>\n      <b>Key Insight:</b> Flow rate scales with the <b>fourth power of radius (r<sup>4</sup>)</b>, illustrating why minor constrictions in human blood vessels cause dramatic surges in blood pressure.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q85: Analyze the dimensional consistency of the Newton-Laplace equation for the speed of sound in a gas: v = √(γ P / ρ). Explain why Newton's isothermal assumption failed and how Laplace corrected it.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Dimensional Verification:</b><br>\n      LHS: Speed of sound [v] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>].<br>\n      RHS: The adiabatic ratio &gamma; = C<sub>p</sub> / C<sub>v</sub> is a pure dimensionless number ([&gamma;] = [1]).<br>\n      Dimensions of Pressure: [P] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      Dimensions of Density: [\rho] = [M L<sup>-3</sup>]<br>\n      Ratio: [P / \rho] = \frac{[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}{[M L<sup>-3</sup>]} = [M<sup>(1-1)</sup> L<sup>(-1+3)</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] = [L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>\n      Taking square root: [(P / \rho)<sup>1/2</sup>] = ([L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>])<sup>1/2</sup> = [L T<sup>-1</sup>].<br>\n      Since [\text{LHS}] = [\text{RHS}] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>], the equation is <b>dimensionally consistent</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Physical Historical Context:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Newton's Assumption (Isothermal Propagation):</b> Newton assumed sound compressions and rarefactions occur slowly, allowing heat to exchange with surroundings, keeping temperature constant (E = P). This predicted v = sqrt{P/\rho} approx 280 \text{ m s}<sup>-1</sup> in air at STP, which was ~16% lower than experimental value (331 \text{ m s}<sup>-1</sup>).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Laplace's Correction (Adiabatic Propagation):</b> In 1816, Laplace recognized that sound vibrations occur with extreme rapidness and air is a poor conductor of heat. Therefore, compressions cause rapid heating and rarefactions cause rapid cooling with zero time for heat exchange (adiabatic process). The adiabatic bulk modulus is E_s = gamma P.<br>\n      &bull; This gave v = sqrt{gamma P / \rho} = sqrt{1.41 \times (1.013 \times 10<sup>5</sup>) / 1.293} = mathbf{331.3 \text{ m s}<sup>-1</sup>}, matching experimental observations perfectly.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q86: Explain how dimensional analysis is applied to convert a physical quantity from one system of units to another. Provide a step-by-step conversion of 100 Watt of electrical power into an arbitrary system whose base units are 10 kg, 100 m, and 1 minute.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Principle of Unit Conversion:</b><br>\n      Physical magnitude Q = n_1 u_1 = n_2 u_2 is invariant under system transitions.<br>\n      If a quantity has dimensional formula [M<sup>a</sup> L<sup>b</sup> T<sup>c</sup>], its unit in System 1 is u_1 = M_1<sup>a</sup> L_1<sup>b</sup> T_1<sup>c</sup> and in System 2 is u_2 = M_2<sup>a</sup> L_2<sup>b</sup> T_2<sup>c</sup>.<br>\n      Master conversion formula:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 16px;\">\n        <b>n<sub>2</sub> = n<sub>1</sub> left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right]<sup>a</sup> left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right]<sup>b</sup> left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right]<sup>c</sup></b>\n      </div>\n      <b>2. Conversion of P = 100 Watt:</b><br>\n      Power = Work / Time implies [P] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup>] implies a = 1, b = 2, c = -3.<br><br>\n      <b>System 1 (SI):</b><br>\n      n_1 = 100<br>\n      M_1 = 1 \text{ kg}, L_1 = 1 \text{ m}, T_1 = 1 \text{ s}<br><br>\n      <b>System 2 (New Custom System):</b><br>\n      M_2 = 10 \text{ kg} implies M_1 / M_2 = 1 / 10 = 10<sup>-1</sup><br>\n      L_2 = 100 \text{ m} implies L_1 / L_2 = 1 / 100 = 10<sup>-2</sup><br>\n      T_2 = 1 \text{ min} = 60 \text{ s} implies T_1 / T_2 = 1 / 60<br><br>\n      <b>Calculation:</b><br>\n      n_2 = 100 \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">10</span></span>\right]<sup>1</sup> \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">100</span></span>\right]<sup>2</sup> \times left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">60</span></span>\right]<sup>-3</sup><br>\n      n_2 = 100 \times 10<sup>-1</sup> \times (10<sup>-2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> \times (60)<sup>3</sup><br>\n      n_2 = 10<sup>2</sup> \times 10<sup>-1</sup> \times 10<sup>-4</sup> \times 216,000<br>\n      n_2 = 10<sup>-3</sup> \times 2.16 \times 10<sup>5</sup> = 2.16 \times 10<sup>2</sup> = mathbf{216}.<br><br>\n      <b>Result:</b> 100 Watt in the new system equals <b>216 new units of power</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q87: Explain why van der Waals' gas equation requires correction terms for ideal gas behavior. State the equation and prove the dimensional formulas and SI units of van der Waals constants a and b.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Origin of Corrections:</b><br>\n      The ideal gas equation P V = n R T assumes:<br>\n      (a) Gas molecules are point masses with negligible finite volume.<br>\n      (b) Intermolecular forces between gas molecules are zero.<br>\n      Johannes Diderik van der Waals corrected these assumptions for real gases:<br>\n      &bull; <b>Volume Correction:</b> Real molecules have a finite incompressible core volume. The free available volume for motion is (V - b), where b represents effective molecular co-volume.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Pressure Correction:</b> Interior molecules attract surface molecules inward, reducing impact force against container walls. Real observed pressure P must be augmented by an internal cohesion pressure term <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br><br>\n      <b>van der Waals Equation:</b>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 16px;\">\n        left(P + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sup>2</sup></span></span>\right) (V - b) = R T quad (\text{for 1 mole})\n      </div>\n      <b>2. Dimensional Proof by Homogeneity:</b><br>\n      <b>For constant a:</b><br>\n      Because <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sup>2</sup></span></span> is added directly to pressure P, by Principle of Homogeneity:<br>\n      [P] = left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sup>2</sup></span></span>\right] implies [a] = [P] [V<sup>2</sup>]<br>\n      [a] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] \times ([L<sup>3</sup>])<sup>2</sup> = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>] [L<sup>6</sup>] = mathbf{[M L<sup>5</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}.<br>\n      <b>SI Unit of a:</b> \text{N m}<sup>4</sup> = \text{kg m}<sup>5</sup> \text{s}<sup>-2</sup> \text{mol}<sup>-2</sup>.<br><br>\n      <b>For constant b:</b><br>\n      Because b is subtracted from molar volume V, it must have the dimensions of volume:<br>\n      [b] = [V] = mathbf{[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]}.<br>\n      <b>SI Unit of b:</b> \text{m}<sup>3</sup> \text{mol}<sup>-1</sup>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q88: Define the four standard natural Planck Units (Planck Length, Planck Mass, Planck Time, Planck Temperature) derived from fundamental constants c, G, h, and k_B. Derive expressions for Planck Length and Planck Mass dimensionally.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Concept of Natural Planck Units:</b><br>\n      In 1899, Max Planck discovered that combinations of fundamental universal constants—speed of light c, gravitational constant G, reduced Planck's constant hbar (or h), and Boltzmann constant k_B—form natural, coordinate-independent units of measurement defining the fundamental quantum gravitational scale of nature.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivation of Planck Length (l_p):</b><br>\n      Let l_p = c<sup>a</sup> G<sup>b</sup> h<sup>c</sup>. Dimensions:<br>\n      [c] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>], [G] = [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>], [h] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>].<br>\n      [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>] = [L T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>a</sup> [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<sup>b</sup> [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>c</sup> = [M<sup>(-b+c)</sup> L<sup>(a+3b+2c)</sup> T<sup>(-a-2b-c)</sup>].<br>\n      Equate powers:<br>\n      (1) -b + c = 0 implies b = c<br>\n      (2) -a - 2b - c = 0 implies -a - 3b = 0 implies a = -3b<br>\n      (3) a + 3b + 2c = 1 implies -3b + 3b + 2b = 1 implies 2b = 1 implies b = 1/2.<br>\n      Hence: c = 1/2, and a = -3(1/2) = -3/2.<br>\n      <b>l<sub>p</sub> = sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">h G</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c<sup>3</sup></span></span>} approx 1.616 \times 10<sup>-35</sup> \text{ m}</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Derivation of Planck Mass (m_p):</b><br>\n      Let m_p = c<sup>a</sup> G<sup>b</sup> h<sup>c</sup>.<br>\n      [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>] = [M<sup>(-b+c)</sup> L<sup>(a+3b+2c)</sup> T<sup>(-a-2b-c)</sup>].<br>\n      (1) -b + c = 1 implies c = b + 1<br>\n      (2) -a - 2b - (b+1) = 0 implies -a - 3b - 1 = 0 implies a = -3b - 1<br>\n      (3) a + 3b + 2c = 0 implies (-3b - 1) + 3b + 2(b + 1) = 0 implies -1 + 2b + 2 = 0 implies 2b = -1 implies b = -1/2.<br>\n      Then c = -1/2 + 1 = 1/2, and a = -3(-1/2) - 1 = 3/2 - 1 = 1/2.<br>\n      <b>m<sub>p</sub> = sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">h c</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G</span></span>} approx 2.176 \times 10<sup>-8</sup> \text{ kg}</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>4. Planck Time & Planck Temperature:</b><br>\n      &bull; Planck Time: t_p = l_p / c = sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">h G</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c<sup>5</sup></span></span>} approx 5.39 \times 10<sup>-44</sup> \text{ s}.<br>\n      &bull; Planck Temperature: T_p = m_p c<sup>2</sup> / k_B = sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">h c<sup>5</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G k_B<sup>2</sup></span></span>} approx 1.417 \times 10<sup>32</sup> \text{ K}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q89: Explain in detail the 2019 Historic Revision of the SI System enacted by the CGPM. Contrast the modern constant-based definitions with historical physical prototypes.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. The Historic Shift in Metrology (May 20, 2019):</b><br>\n      From 1889 to 2019, the kilogram was defined by a physical cylinder of platinum-iridium kept in a triple-vault at the BIPM in S&egrave;vres, France (the <i>International Prototype of the Kilogram - IPK</i>, or \"Le Grand K\"). Periodic comparisons showed that copies had drifted by ~50 micrograms due to surface contamination and cleaning wear. Basing modern quantum civilization on a single physical artifact prone to microgram drift was unacceptable.<br><br>\n      <b>2. The Seven Defining Constants of Nature:</b><br>\n      On World Metrology Day (May 20, 2019), the 26th CGPM officially redefined the entire SI system by fixing the exact numerical values of 7 physical constants with <b>zero measurement uncertainty</b>:<br>\n      &bull; Hyperfine transition frequency of Caesium-133: Delta\nu_{Cs} = 9,192,631,770 \text{ s}<sup>-1</sup> (fixes <b>Second</b>).<br>\n      &bull; Speed of light in vacuum: c = 299,792,458 \text{ m s}<sup>-1</sup> (fixes <b>Meter</b>).<br>\n      &bull; Planck constant: h = 6.62607015 \times 10<sup>-34</sup> \text{ J s} = \text{kg m}<sup>2</sup> \text{s}<sup>-1</sup> (fixes <b>Kilogram</b> via Kibble balance).<br>\n      &bull; Elementary charge: e = 1.602176634 \times 10<sup>-19</sup> \text{ C} = \text{A s} (fixes <b>Ampere</b>).<br>\n      &bull; Boltzmann constant: k_B = 1.380649 \times 10<sup>-23</sup> \text{ J K}<sup>-1</sup> (fixes <b>Kelvin</b>).<br>\n      &bull; Avogadro constant: N_A = 6.02214076 \times 10<sup>23</sup> \text{ mol}<sup>-1</sup> (fixes <b>Mole</b>).<br>\n      &bull; Luminous efficacy of 540 THz radiation: K_{cd} = 683 \text{ lm W}<sup>-1</sup> (fixes <b>Candela</b>).<br><br>\n      <b>3. Scientific Advantages:</b><br>\n      (a) Standard units are now permanent, immutable, and accessible anywhere in the universe without physical artifacts.<br>\n      (b) Enables direct realization of base units at microscopic quantum scales (e.g. quantum Hall effect, Josephson effect).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q90: Explain the classification of physical quantities into Dimensional Variables, Dimensionless Variables, Dimensional Constants, and Dimensionless Constants with 3 examples and physical interpretations of each.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      Physical quantities are classified systematically based on whether they vary or remain fixed, and whether they possess dimensions:<br><br>\n      <b>1. Dimensional Variables:</b><br>\n      Quantities that change in magnitude from one situation to another and possess non-zero dimensions.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Velocity ([L T<sup>-1</sup>]):</i> Varies with speed and direction.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Acceleration ([L T<sup>-2</sup>]):</i> Changes during non-uniform motion.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Force ([M L T<sup>-2</sup>]):</i> Varies according to mass and acceleration.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Dimensionless Variables:</b><br>\n      Quantities that take variable numerical values depending on experimental conditions, but represent pure dimensionless ratios [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].<br>\n      &bull; <i>Refractive Index (&mu; = c / v):</i> Ratio of two velocities, varies across media.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Mechanical Strain (&Delta;L / L):</i> Ratio of elongation to original length, varies with stress.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Specific Gravity (&rho;<sub>substance</sub> / &rho;<sub>water</sub>):</i> Ratio of densities.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Dimensional Constants:</b><br>\n      Universal or empirical parameters that hold constant numerical values throughout nature but carry physical dimensions.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Universal Gravitational Constant G:</i> 6.674 \times 10<sup>-11</sup> \text{ N m}<sup>2</sup> \text{kg}<sup>-2</sup>, dimensions [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].<br>\n      &bull; <i>Planck's Constant h:</i> 6.626 \times 10<sup>-34</sup> \text{ J s}, dimensions [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>].<br>\n      &bull; <i>Speed of light in vacuum c:</i> 2.998 \times 10<sup>8</sup> \text{ m s}<sup>-1</sup>, dimensions [L T<sup>-1</sup>].<br><br>\n      <b>4. Dimensionless Constants:</b><br>\n      Universal mathematical or geometric numbers that have invariant fixed values and zero physical dimensions [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>].<br>\n      &bull; <i>Pi (&pi; &approx; 3.14159...):</i> Ratio of circle circumference to diameter.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Euler's number (e &approx; 2.71828...):</i> Base of natural logarithms.<br>\n      &bull; <i>Pure integers and rational numbers:</i> 1, 2, 1/2, 4pi, etc.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q91: Explain why dimensional analysis is powerless to derive the formula for the distance traveled by a projectile or vehicle with initial velocity: s = u t + ½ a t². Detail 4 other critical limitations of dimensional analysis.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Inability on Multi-Term Additive Expressions:</b><br>\n      Dimensional analysis operates through multiplicative power laws: Q = k A<sup>a</sup> B<sup>b</sup> C<sup>c</sup>. In the equation s = ut + ½ at<sup>2</sup>, there are two separate additive terms on the right side (ut and ½ at<sup>2</sup>). Dimensional analysis cannot generate the plus sign (+), nor can it deduce multiple independent numerical factors (the factor 1 for ut and the factor ½ for at<sup>2</sup>) simultaneously. It can only check if each individual term is dimensionally homogeneous with displacement [L].<br><br>\n      <b>2. Four Other Fundamental Limitations:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Limitation 1 &bull; Proportionality Constants (k):</b> Dimensional analysis gives no information regarding whether k = 1, 2pi, 1/2, or any other numerical coefficient. Constants must be found through experimental measurements or analytical calculus derivations.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Limitation 2 &bull; Breakdown for More Than 3 Variables in Mechanics:</b> In classical mechanics, dimensional equations yield only 3 independent simultaneous equations (from powers of M, L, T). If a physical phenomenon depends upon 4 or more independent variables, we obtain an underdetermined system with infinitely many mathematical solutions.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Limitation 3 &bull; Transcendental Functions:</b> Trigonometric (sin \theta, cos omega t), exponential (e<sup>-lambda t</sup>), and logarithmic (ln x) functions must have dimensionless arguments. Dimensional analysis cannot deduce formulas containing such mathematical functions.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Limitation 4 &bull; Inability to Distinguish Scalars from Vectors:</b> Two fundamentally distinct physical quantities can have identical dimensions. For example, <b>Work</b> (scalar dot product \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{d}) and <b>Torque</b> (vector cross product \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F}) both possess dimensions [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]. Dimensional analysis cannot tell whether a calculated quantity is scalar or vector.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q92: State the rules for significant figures in scientific notation. Explain why expressing measurements in standard scientific notation (N × 10ⁿ) eliminates all ambiguity regarding trailing zeros.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Ambiguity of Trailing Zeros in Integers:</b><br>\n      In numbers without decimal points, such as 45,000 \text{ m}, it is impossible to discern whether the trailing zeros are significant measuring digits or merely placeholders indicating magnitude. Depending on instrument precision:<br>\n      &bull; If measured to the nearest 1000 m, it has 2 significant figures.<br>\n      &bull; If measured to the nearest 100 m, it has 3 significant figures.<br>\n      &bull; If measured to the nearest 1 m, it has 5 significant figures.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Scientific Notation Resolves All Ambiguity:</b><br>\n      In standard scientific notation, every measurement is expressed in the form:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 16px;\"><b>N &times; 10<sup>n</sup></b></div>\n      where 1 le N < 10 and n is an integer exponent.<br>\n      <b>Universal Rule:</b> All digits in the coefficient N (including all trailing zeros after the decimal) are strictly significant. The exponential factor 10<sup>n</sup> carries zero significant figures.<br><br>\n      <b>Illustrative Breakdown:</b><br>\n      &bull; 4.5 \times 10<sup>4</sup> \text{ m} implies <b>2 significant figures</b> (least count 1000 m).<br>\n      &bull; 4.50 \times 10<sup>4</sup> \text{ m} implies <b>3 significant figures</b> (least count 100 m).<br>\n      &bull; 4.500 \times 10<sup>4</sup> \text{ m} implies <b>4 significant figures</b> (least count 10 m).<br>\n      &bull; 4.5000 \times 10<sup>4</sup> \text{ m} implies <b>5 significant figures</b> (least count 1 m).<br>\n      Thus, scientific notation displays the exact precision and least count of the instrument unambiguously.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q93: Derive the dimensional formula for Coefficient of Thermal Conductivity (K) from the fundamental equation of heat conduction. Deduce its SI and CGS units and conversion factor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Governing Law of Heat Conduction:</b><br>\n      By Fourier's Law of heat conduction, heat energy Q flowing normally across a slab of cross-sectional area A, thickness d, with face temperatures T_1 and T_2 in time t is:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0;\">\n        Q = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">K A (T<sub>1</sub> - T<sub>2</sub>) t</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span></span> implies K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Q d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A (T<sub>1</sub> - T<sub>2</sub>) t</span></span>\n      </div>\n      <b>2. Dimensional Derivation:</b><br>\n      &bull; Heat energy: [Q] = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      &bull; Thickness: [d] = [L]<br>\n      &bull; Cross-sectional area: [A] = [L<sup>2</sup>]<br>\n      &bull; Temperature difference: [T_1 - T_2] = [Theta] (or [K])<br>\n      &bull; Time interval: [t] = [T]<br><br>\n      Substitute into equation for K:<br>\n      [K] = \frac{[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>][L]}{[L<sup>2</sup>][Theta][T]} = \frac{[M L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}{[L<sup>2</sup> T Theta]} = mathbf{[M L T<sup>-3</sup> Theta<sup>-1</sup>]}.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Units & Conversion Factor:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> \text{J s}<sup>-1</sup> \text{m}<sup>-1</sup> \text{K}<sup>-1</sup> = mathbf{\text{W m}<sup>-1</sup> \text{K}<sup>-1</sup>}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>CGS Unit:</b> \text{cal s}<sup>-1</sup> \text{cm}<sup>-1</sup> \text{}<sup>c</sup>irc\text{C}<sup>-1</sup>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Conversion Factor:</b><br>\n      1 \text{ cal s}<sup>-1</sup> \text{cm}<sup>-1</sup> \text{}<sup>c</sup>irc\text{C}<sup>-1</sup> = \frac{4.184 \text{ J}}{(1 \text{ s})(10<sup>-2</sup> \text{ m})(1 \text{ K})} = 4.184 \times 10<sup>2</sup> \text{ W m}<sup>-1</sup> \text{K}<sup>-1</sup> = mathbf{418.4 \text{ W m}<sup>-1</sup> \text{K}<sup>-1</sup>}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q94: Explain the concept of Least Count of an instrument. Contrast the least count, measurement resolution, and precision of a Meter Scale, Vernier Caliper, and Screw Gauge.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Definition of Least Count (LC):</b><br>\n      The Least Count of a measuring instrument is the smallest physical magnitude that can be measured directly, accurately, and reliably by the instrument without estimation.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Detailed Instrument Comparison:</b><br>\n      <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 12px 0;\">\n        <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px;\">\n          <thead>\n            <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; color: #FF512F;\">\n              <th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Instrument</th>\n              <th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Least Count (LC)</th>\n              <th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Significant Decimals (cm)</th>\n              <th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Precision Level</th>\n            </tr>\n          </thead>\n          <tbody>\n            <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\"><b>Meter Scale</b></td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">1 mm = 0.1 cm</td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">1 decimal place (e.g. 5.4 cm)</td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Lowest precision</td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\"><b>Vernier Caliper</b></td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">0.1 mm = 0.01 cm</td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">2 decimal places (e.g. 5.42 cm)</td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Moderate precision</td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\"><b>Screw Gauge / Spherometer</b></td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">0.01 mm = 0.001 cm</td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">3 decimal places (e.g. 5.426 cm)</td>\n              <td style=\"padding: 8px;\">Highest precision</td>\n            </tr>\n          </tbody>\n        </table>\n      </div>\n      <b>Physical Takeaway:</b> An experimental measurement recorded as 5.400 \text{ cm} immediately communicates to any scientist that the instrument used had a least count of 0.001 \text{ cm} (Screw Gauge), whereas 5.4 \text{ cm} indicates a crude meter scale.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q95: Explain why solid angle is measured in steradians. Derive the relationship between the plane semi-vertical angle (θ) of a right circular cone and its subtended solid angle (Ω = 2π(1 - cos θ)).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Concept of Solid Angle:</b><br>\n      While plane angle measures 2D divergence between two lines, solid angle measures 3D conical divergence radiating outwards from a point into spatial surroundings, defined as Omega = int \frac{dA cos alpha}{r<sup>2</sup>}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivation for a Right Circular Cone of Semi-Vertical Angle &theta;:</b><br>\n      Consider a sphere of radius r centered at the apex of a cone. Take a thin spherical ring strip of angular radius phi and angular thickness dphi subtended on the spherical cap (0 le phi le \theta):<br>\n      &bull; Radius of circular ring: y = r sin phi<br>\n      &bull; Width of circular ring: r , dphi<br>\n      &bull; Surface area of ring strip: dA = 2pi y (r , dphi) = 2pi (r sin phi) (r , dphi) = 2pi r<sup>2</sup> sin phi , dphi.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Integration Over the Entire Cone:</b><br>\n      Total solid angle Omega = int \frac{dA}{r<sup>2</sup>} = int_0\theta \frac{2pi r<sup>2</sup> sin phi , dphi}{r<sup>2</sup>} = 2pi int_0\theta sin phi , dphi.<br>\n      Omega = 2pi [-cos phi]_0\theta = 2pi (-cos \theta - (-cos 0)) = 2pi (1 - cos \theta).\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        Omega = 2pi (1 - cos \theta) \text{ steradians}\n      </div>\n      <b>Boundary Verification:</b><br>\n      &bull; For a flat surface (hemisphere): \theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc implies Omega = 2pi(1 - 0) = 2pi \text{ sr}.<br>\n      &bull; For a full closed sphere: \theta = 180<sup>c</sup>irc implies Omega = 2pi(1 - (-1)) = 4pi \text{ sr} (matches Gauss's law!).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q96: A physical quantity X is given by X = (a² b³ / (c √d)). If the percentage errors in the measurement of a, b, c, and d are 1%, 3%, 2%, and 4% respectively, calculate the maximum fractional and percentage error in X.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Fractional Error Formula:</b><br>\n      Given: X = \frac{a<sup>2</sup> b<sup>3</sup>}{c sqrt{d}} = a<sup>2</sup> b<sup>3</sup> c<sup>-1</sup> d<sup>-1/2</sup>.<br>\n      Taking natural logarithm on both sides:<br>\n      ln X = 2 ln a + 3 ln b - ln c - \frac{1}{2} ln d.<br>\n      Differentiating to find maximum relative uncertainty (all errors add in worst-case analysis):<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0;\">\n        <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;X</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">X</span></span> = 2 left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span></span>\right) + 3 left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;b</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">b</span></span>\right) + 1 left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;c</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span>\right) + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span></span>\right)\n      </div>\n      <b>2. Percentage Error Calculation:</b><br>\n      Multiply each term by 100%:<br>\n      % \text{ error in } X = left[ 2 left(\frac{Delta a}{a} \times 100\right) + 3 left(\frac{Delta b}{b} \times 100\right) + 1 left(\frac{Delta c}{c} \times 100\right) + \frac{1}{2} left(\frac{Delta d}{d} \times 100\right) \right]<br>\n      Substitute given percentage values:<br>\n      % \text{ error in } X = [2(1%) + 3(3%) + 1(2%) + \frac{1}{2}(4%)]<br>\n      % \text{ error in } X = [2% + 9% + 2% + 2%] = mathbf{15%}.<br><br>\n      <b>Maximum Fractional Error:</b> \frac{Delta X}{X} = \frac{15}{100} = mathbf{0.15}.<br>\n      <b>Maximum Percentage Error:</b> mathbf{pm 15%}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q97: Find the dimensional formula of Magnetic Field (B), Magnetic Flux (Φ_B), and Magnetic Dipole Moment (M).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Magnetic Field (B):</b><br>\n      From Lorentz force equation: F = q v B sin \theta implies B = \frac{F}{q v}.<br>\n      Dimensions: [B] = \frac{[M L T<sup>-2</sup>]}{[A T][L T<sup>-1</sup>]} = \frac{[M L T<sup>-2</sup>]}{[A L]} = mathbf{[M L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]}.<br>\n      SI Unit: <b>Tesla (T) = N A<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Magnetic Flux (&Phi;<sub>B</sub>):</b><br>\n      Phi_B = \u000bec{B} cdot \u000bec{A} implies [Phi_B] = [B][A] = [M L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>][L<sup>2</sup>] = mathbf{[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]}.<br>\n      SI Unit: <b>Weber (Wb) = T m<sup>2</sup></b>.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Magnetic Dipole Moment (M):</b><br>\n      M = I \times A implies [M] = [I][A] = [A][L<sup>2</sup>] = mathbf{[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>0</sup> A<sup>1</sup>]}.<br>\n      SI Unit: <b>A m<sup>2</sup> = J T<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q98: Show dimensionally that the expressions for Energy Density of Electric Field (u_E = ½ ε₀ E²) and Magnetic Field (u_B = B² / (2 μ₀)) have identical dimensions of Pressure [M L⁻¹ T⁻²].</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Electric Energy Density (u_E = ½ \u000barepsilon_0 E<sup>2</sup>):</b><br>\n      Dimensions of \u000barepsilon_0: [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>]<br>\n      Dimensions of Electric Field E = F/q: [M L T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]<br>\n      [E<sup>2</sup>] = [M<sup>2</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-6</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      [u_E] = [\u000barepsilon_0][E<sup>2</sup>] = [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>] \times [M<sup>2</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-6</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>] = [M<sup>(-1+2)</sup> L<sup>(-3+2)</sup> T<sup>(4-6)</sup> A<sup>(2-2)</sup>] = mathbf{[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Magnetic Energy Density (u_B = B<sup>2</sup> / (2 mu_0)):</b><br>\n      Dimensions of B: [M L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>] implies [B<sup>2</sup>] = [M<sup>2</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-4</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      Dimensions of mu_0: [M L T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]<br>\n      [u_B] = \frac{[B<sup>2</sup>]}{[mu_0]} = \frac{[M<sup>2</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-4</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]}{[M L T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]} = [M<sup>(2-1)</sup> L<sup>(0-1)</sup> T<sup>(-4 - (-2))</sup> A<sup>(-2 - (-2))</sup>] = mathbf{[M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}.<br><br>\n      <b>Conclusion:</b> Both u_E and u_B have dimensions [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>], which is identically the dimensional formula of <b>Pressure</b> (\text{Force}/\text{Area} = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>]/[L<sup>2</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]) and <b>Energy per unit volume</b> (\text{J}/\text{m}<sup>3</sup> = [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]/[L<sup>3</sup>] = [M L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q99: Explain why dimensional analysis cannot distinguish between physical quantities that have identical dimensions, such as Work and Torque. Discuss the mathematical and physical nature of both quantities.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Identical Dimensional Formulation:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Work:</b> Work is defined as the scalar (dot) product of force and displacement vectors: W = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{d} = F d cos \theta.<br>\n      Dimensions: [W] = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>][L] = mathbf{[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Torque:</b> Torque is defined as the vector (cross) product of position vector and force vector: \u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F}, with magnitude \tau = r F sin \theta.<br>\n      Dimensions: [\tau] = [L][M L T<sup>-2</sup>] = mathbf{[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Why Dimensional Analysis Fails:</b><br>\n      Dimensional analysis concerns itself solely with base physical quantities (Mass, Length, Time). It is fundamentally blind to spatial directionality, tensor rank, and vector characteristics:<br>\n      (a) <b>Scalar vs Vector Nature:</b> Work is a true scalar (rank-0 tensor) representing energy transfer. Torque is an axial vector (pseudovector, rank-1 tensor) representing rotational moment.<br>\n      (b) <b>Units Convention:</b> In the SI system, Work is designated in <b>Joules (J)</b>, while Torque is strictly designated in <b>Newton-meters (N m)</b> to prevent physical confusion. Dimensional analysis cannot detect this conceptual divergence.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q100: Provide an exhaustive master synthesis of Unit I: Physical World and Measurement (05 Marks), summarizing its core concepts, formulas, dimensional constants, and board exam focus areas.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Comprehensive Derivation & Step-by-Step Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Comprehensive Chapter Summary:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Foundations:</b> Physical quantities require both magnitude and unit: Q = n_1 u_1 = n_2 u_2, establishing n propto 1/u.<br>\n      &bull; <b>SI System:</b> Formalized by 14th CGPM in 1971 and redefined in 2019 using 7 defining physical constants (c, h, e, k_B, N_A, Delta\nu_{Cs}, K_{cd}). Comprises 7 base units (m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd) and 2 supplementary dimensionless units: Radian (rad, [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]) and Steradian (sr, [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Significant Figures:</b> Quantify experimental certainty. Governed by 6 core rules. In addition/subtraction, least decimal places govern; in multiplication/division, least significant figures govern.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Dimensional Homogeneity:</b> Only terms with identical dimensions can be added, subtracted, or equated ([A] = [B] = [C] for A + B = C).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Three Core Applications:</b> (1) Checking consistency of equations, (2) Deducing physical relations among variables via power laws, (3) Converting units using n_2 = n_1 [M_1/M_2]<sup>a</sup> [L_1/L_2]<sup>b</sup> [T_1/T_2]<sup>c</sup>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Core Limitations:</b> Inability to determine dimensionless constants (k), failure for transcendental functions, inability to handle equations with more than 3 variables in mechanics, and insensitivity to vector vs scalar distinctions.<br><br>\n      <b>2. High-Yield Board Exam Topics:</b><br>\n      (a) van der Waals constants a and b dimensions ([a] = [M L<sup>5</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>], [b] = [L<sup>3</sup>]).<br>\n      (b) Conversions of 1 J to 10<sup>7</sup> Erg, 1 N to 10<sup>5</sup> Dyne, and G from SI to CGS.<br>\n      (c) Derivations of simple pendulum T = 2pisqrt{l/g}, Stokes' law F = 6pieta r v, and centripetal force F = m v<sup>2</sup> / r.<br>\n      (d) Dimensional constants like h, G, \u000barepsilon_0, mu_0 and proof that c = 1/sqrt{\u000barepsilon_0 mu_0}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

